window.__hvBdRegister("seneca-epistles", {"key":"seneca-epistles","sections":[{"i":0,"j":9,"loc":"To Marcia, on Consolation (1 of 6)","title":"Opening to Marcia: Octavia and Livia","summary":"After editorial notes on the collection, on Seneca's exile to Corsica and on the occasion of the work, Seneca addresses Marcia, still mourning her son Metilius after three years. He praises her courage when her father Cremutius Cordus died under Sejanus and her publication of his burned histories, then announces that he will fight her grief by force, since it has hardened into habit. He sets before her two examples from the imperial house: Octavia, who mourned Marcellus all her life and shut out all comfort, and Livia, who buried Drusus and then lived gladly with his memory. Marcia is asked to choose which model to follow.","ideas":["Grief left too long becomes a habit the mind takes morbid pleasure in","Marcia's earlier strength over her father's death proves her wound can heal","Octavia's endless mourning against Livia's measured grief and cherished memory"],"terms":[{"t":"consolatio (consolation)","d":"A literary letter or essay offering philosophical arguments against grief to a bereaved person."}]},{"i":10,"j":17,"loc":"To Marcia, on Consolation (2 of 6)","title":"Areus counsels Livia; grief is not natural","summary":"Seneca reports the philosopher Areus's address to Livia: she should keep her blameless conduct in mourning, let friends speak of Drusus, and dwell on her joys rather than his death. Seneca applies this to Marcia and argues that if tears cannot undo fate, grief should end. He grants that some pang is natural but argues that prolonged mourning comes from opinion, not nature: animals grieve briefly, and grief varies by sex, culture and learning, whereas natural forces act on all alike. Grief also fades with time, which a natural passion would not; persistent mourning arises because people never expect misfortune to strike them.","ideas":["Speaking of the dead son is a pleasure, not a wound","Natural feelings are uniform and do not fade; grief is neither","Foreseen blows fall more lightly: what befalls one may befall all"],"terms":[{"t":"praemeditatio (foreseeing evils)","d":"The practice of rehearsing possible misfortunes in advance so that they lose their sting."}]},{"i":18,"j":24,"loc":"To Marcia, on Consolation (3 of 6)","title":"Life's goods on loan; examples of bereaved fathers","summary":"Seneca tells Marcia that children, office and wealth are furniture lent by Fortune, to be returned without complaint, and that we should love what we have as things about to leave us. He describes human frailty: man is a potter's vessel, killed by a hiccup, yet full of far-reaching plans. He asks whether she grieves for her own loss or her son's, and argues that brief joy in a fine son is better than none. He then lists Romans who bore the loss of children with composure: Sulla, the priest Pulvillus at the Capitol's dedication, Aemilius Paulus at his triumph, Bibulus, Julius Caesar, Augustus and Tiberius.","ideas":["All external goods are borrowed and may be recalled at any hour","Know thyself means knowing one's mortality and frailty","Great men resumed public duties soon after burying their children"],"terms":[{"t":"Fortuna (Fortune)","d":"The capricious power Seneca portrays as lending and seizing all external goods without regard to desert."}]},{"i":25,"j":29,"loc":"To Marcia, on Consolation (4 of 6)","title":"Brave women, the voyage to Syracuse, life's terms","summary":"Answering the objection that he cites only men, Seneca insists women have equal mental power and cites Lucretia, Cloelia and the two Cornelias, including the mother of the Gracchi. He tells Marcia that Fortune has left her two daughters and granddaughters by Metilius, and compares her to a farmer replanting after a storm. He then imagines a traveller warned beforehand of both the wonders and the dangers of Syracuse, and Nature giving parents the same warning about children. Finally he extends the image to birth itself: entering a city of gods and men full of marvels and of pains, whose conditions one accepts by living.","ideas":["Women have the same capacity for virtue and endurance as men","Those left alive should recall the lost son, not the grief","Life, like a voyage, is taken on known terms; the gods promised nothing"],"terms":[]},{"i":30,"j":36,"loc":"To Marcia, on Consolation (5 of 6)","title":"Death is no evil; Cordus's death","summary":"Seneca argues that what torments mourners is an idea, and every evil is as great as we judge it. The dead suffer nothing: tales of the underworld are poets' fables, and death returns us to the rest before birth, being neither good nor bad. He praises death as Nature's best discovery, freeing slaves and captives, and shows how Pompey, Cicero and Cato would have fared better dying sooner. Against the complaint of an untimely death, he replies that all lives are a point beside eternity and each has a fixed term. He ends by recalling how Cordus starved himself to escape Sejanus while his accusers tried to stop him dying.","ideas":["Grief rests on opinion, so the remedy lies in our own hands","Death is the end of all pains, not a punishment","No one dies before his time; each term is fixed at birth","A timely death can spare a man later disgrace"],"terms":[{"t":"Sejanus","d":"Tiberius's powerful prefect, whose agents drove Marcia's father Cordus to suicide."}]},{"i":37,"j":40,"loc":"To Marcia, on Consolation (6 of 6)","title":"The son's soul ascends; Cordus speaks from heaven","summary":"Seneca argues that souls that leave the body early carry fewest stains and rise most easily, citing Plato on the wise man's longing for death, and says that what is perfect is ripe for dissolution. He urges Marcia to reckon her son's age by his virtues: his devotion to her, his chastity, his priesthood. What lies in the tomb is only bones; his soul, purified, has joined the blessed, where his grandfather Cordus teaches him the stars. In a closing prosopopoeia Cordus speaks from heaven, asks why she mourns so long, and describes the destruction and renewal of the world in fire, in which even blessed souls return to their elements.","ideas":["The brightest flames burn out soonest","The body is chains and darkness to the soul","Cordus's vision of the heavens and of the universe's periodic end"],"terms":[{"t":"ekpyrōsis (world conflagration)","d":"The Stoic doctrine that the universe ends in fire and is then reconstructed anew."}]},{"i":41,"j":48,"loc":"To My Mother Helvia, on Consolation (1 of 6)","title":"Seneca resolves to console his mother","summary":"After an editorial introduction on the work's occasion, Seneca writes from exile to his mother Helvia. He explains his delay: grief must not be treated too early, and he knew of no one who consoled his family while they mourned him. Choosing a harsh remedy, he lists her past sorrows: her mother's death at her birth, a stepmother, the loss of an uncle and husband, three grandchildren and Seneca's own child, and now his exile. He then sets out his plan: to show first that he himself is not unhappy, and then that her own misfortune is not severe.","ideas":["Medicine applied too soon inflames a wound","A mind scarred by many sorrows should not bewail one more","Twofold plan: Seneca is not wretched, and her loss is bearable"],"terms":[]},{"i":49,"j":51,"loc":"To My Mother Helvia, on Consolation (2 of 6)","title":"Exile as mere change of place","summary":"Seneca says Nature lets everyone be happy by himself, and that he never trusted Fortune's gifts, keeping them at a distance so their loss did not wound him. He defines exile as a change of place, with poverty and disgrace to be treated later. Rome is full of people who left their homelands, and even barren Corsica has more strangers than natives. The restless human mind, made of heavenly spirit, shares the perpetual motion of the stars. Whole nations have migrated, Rome itself was founded by an exile, and Corsica has changed its inhabitants many times.","ideas":["No one loses by Fortune's frowns who was not deceived by her smiles","Most people in every city live away from their native land","Migration of peoples is perpetual; nothing stays where it was born"],"terms":[]},{"i":52,"j":54,"loc":"To My Mother Helvia, on Consolation (3 of 6)","title":"Nature and virtue go with the exile","summary":"Seneca combines Varro's thought that the exile meets the same Nature everywhere with Brutus's that he takes his virtues with him: the world and a mind to contemplate it cannot be taken away. From any soil he can study the heavens. A cottage entered by the virtues is finer than a temple, as Brutus saw in Marcellus living contentedly in exile at Mytilene. Turning to poverty, Seneca argues the body needs little and all beyond is vice; he attacks Roman gluttony, Caligula's costly dinner and Apicius, who poisoned himself with ten million sesterces left.","ideas":["Common Nature and one's own virtue accompany one into exile","All the world is the wise man's country","Nature is satisfied with little; covetous desire never is"],"terms":[{"t":"Apicius","d":"A notorious gourmand who killed himself fearing poverty on a still vast fortune."}]},{"i":55,"j":57,"loc":"To My Mother Helvia, on Consolation (4 of 6)","title":"Poverty and disgrace cannot touch the mind","summary":"Seneca argues that wants caused by vice can never be satisfied, while one who keeps within nature's limits never feels poor; it is the mind that makes men rich, and the free-born spirit, akin to the gods, can never truly be exiled. The poor are no sadder than the rich, and Homer, Plato, Zeno, Menenius Agrippa, Regulus and Scipio lived or died poor with honour. To the objection that exile combines several evils, he replies that virtue that resists one resists all, since reason conquers vices together. Disgrace cannot touch a man who does not despise himself, as Socrates, Cato and Aristides showed.","ideas":["Vicious desire is a disease like an unquenchable thirst","The body may be exiled, the spirit never","Reason defeats all vices at once or none","No one is despised by others unless he first despises himself"],"terms":[]},{"i":58,"j":61,"loc":"To My Mother Helvia, on Consolation (5 of 6)","title":"Helvia's own grief and the refuge of philosophy","summary":"Seneca turns to Helvia's grief for herself: she cannot miss his protection, having never used her sons for gain, so her sorrow is longing for his presence, made sharper because she left Rome just before his ruin. Womanhood is no excuse, since she has none of the vices of her sex; ancestral custom limited women's mourning, and Cornelia and Rutilia bore losses bravely. Since grief resists our will, he rejects distractions like travel or business, which only cheat sorrow. He urges her instead to return to the liberal studies she began, regretting that his father limited her learning: philosophy alone guards against Fortune.","ideas":["The middle course is to feel regret and also restrain it","Grief cheated by distraction returns; grief conquered by reason is gone","Philosophy is the true refuge from Fortune"],"terms":[]},{"i":62,"j":64,"loc":"To My Mother Helvia, on Consolation (6 of 6)","title":"Family comforts and Seneca's contented exile","summary":"Until philosophy takes hold, Seneca points Helvia to her remaining comforts: his two brothers, one in high office and one in quiet retirement; his nephew Marcus; Novatilla, whom she should raise; and her own father. Her chief comfort is her sister, who nursed Seneca, sought his quaestorship and once, though shipwrecked, saved her drowned husband's body for burial. Seneca praises her modesty during sixteen years in Egypt. He closes by telling his mother to think of him as cheerful, his mind freed from business to study the earth, sea, weather and finally things divine.","ideas":["Seneca prays that Fate's cruelty fall on him alone","His aunt's courage in the shipwreck surpasses Alcestis","Exile gives the mind leisure to contemplate nature and the divine"],"terms":[]},{"i":65,"j":72,"loc":"To Polybius, on Consolation (1 of 4)","title":"All things perish; complaint against Fortune","summary":"After editorial notes on Polybius, Claudius's literary secretary, and the work's flattery, the text begins mid-sentence, its opening lost. Seneca argues that everything made by mortals, and even the universe, will perish, so no single death should be wept over; the common lot is itself a consolation. Since grief helps neither the dead nor the living, he joins Polybius in a lament to Fortune, which found his only vulnerable point in his brother. He praises the dead youth's modesty, then concludes that Fate cannot be moved and that tears, needed so often in life, should be husbanded.","ideas":["Everything that has a beginning has an end","Polybius's wealth, friends, fame and works are beyond Fortune's reach","Causes for weeping never fail, so tears should be sparing"],"terms":[]},{"i":73,"j":78,"loc":"To Polybius, on Consolation (2 of 4)","title":"Duties of example, Caesar and study","summary":"Seneca argues that the dead brother would not want Polybius tortured by grief, and that Polybius must set his brothers an example, like a general hiding dismay. His high position means all eyes watch how he bears this wound; a great fortune is a great slavery. He should remember Caesar, to whom he owes himself entirely, and while Caesar lives he has lost nothing. At home he should fill his hours with study: Homer and Virgil, a history of Claudius's deeds, perhaps Aesop's fables. Finally he should ask whether he grieves for himself or his brother, who is either free from all ill or blessed among the stars.","ideas":["Grief is offensive to the very person it is meant to honour","Public eminence forbids private indulgence in sorrow","The dead are either nonexistent or happy, so weeping is madness or envy"],"terms":[]},{"i":79,"j":83,"loc":"To Polybius, on Consolation (3 of 4)","title":"A loan repaid; praise of Claudius","summary":"Seneca says Polybius should be grateful for having had a fine brother, since past pleasures are secure possessions and Nature gave him only as a loan. Death is called sudden only because people forget mortality; he cites the father who said he knew his son would die. Polybius has brothers, wife and son left, and above all Caesar, whom Seneca praises lavishly, praying for his long life. Seneca speaks of his own hope of recall through Claudius's clemency, then imagines Claudius consoling Polybius with examples of Scipio Africanus and Scipio Aemilianus mourning brothers.","ideas":["Remembered pleasures are truer than present ones","Life is a journey toward death; nothing is new in dying","Seneca hopes Claudius's clemency will end his exile"],"terms":[{"t":"clementia (clemency)","d":"The ruler's mercy, which Seneca praises in Claudius and hopes will restore him."}]},{"i":84,"j":88,"loc":"To Polybius, on Consolation (4 of 4)","title":"Imperial examples and measured grief","summary":"The imagined Claudius continues with brothers parted by death: the Luculli, the Pompeii, Augustus, Gaius and Lucius Caesar, Tiberius and Drusus, Mark Antony, and his own brother Germanicus. Seneca urges Polybius to imitate these, contrasting Caligula, who mourned Drusilla by gambling and cruelty. Polybius should retreat into his studies and write a memoir of his brother, a monument no time destroys. Seneca rejects the harsh view that the wise never mourn: reason should only trim excessive grief, letting tears flow and then cease. He closes apologising that exile among barbarous speech has dulled his writing.","ideas":["Fortune spares not even the house of the gods","Literary memorials outlast stone and marble","Reason cuts off superfluous grief but does not forbid all grief"],"terms":[]},{"i":89,"j":233,"loc":"The Moral Epistles; Introduction","title":"The Moral Epistles: contents and translator's introduction","summary":"A short note presents the Epistulae morales as 124 letters to Lucilius, procurator of Sicily, written near the end of Seneca's life and full of sayings of Epicurus; a list of the letters follows. Gummere's introduction then traces Seneca's career: birth at Corduba, the bar, exile to Corsica, recall by Agrippina as tutor to Nero, partnership with Burrus, and suicide in 65. It describes Lucilius as a self-made Campanian knight with Epicurean leanings, dates the letters to 63–65, and calls them essays more than letters, each opening from a concrete incident. It closes by praising their humane themes (slaves, the arena, marriage) and naming Hense's text as the basis.","ideas":["The letters are moral essays in epistolary form, addressed to Lucilius","Seneca's life—exile, ministry under Nero, forced suicide—frames the work","Each letter moves from a concrete occasion to an abstract theme","Gummere stresses Seneca's humane, 'modern' concerns"],"terms":[{"t":"consolatio","d":"The literary genre of consolation for loss, which Gummere names among the letters' traditional types."},{"t":"diatribe","d":"The pointed popular-philosophical lecture style that Gummere sees shaping the letters' language."}]},{"i":234,"j":252,"loc":"Letters I–IV","title":"Saving time, focused reading, friendship, fear of death","summary":"Letter I urges Lucilius to claim and save his time, the only thing truly ours, since we die daily and most of death lies behind us. Letter II warns against restless, scattered reading: dwell on a few master thinkers and digest one thought each day, here Epicurus' praise of contented poverty. Letter III insists a friend must be judged before admission but then trusted fully, and that both trusting everyone and trusting no one are faults. Letter IV argues that death, the last evil, is not great, that anyone can take a life, and that nature's needs are small.","ideas":["Time is the one possession nature gives us, and it slips away","Few books thoroughly digested beat many skimmed","Judge before befriending, then trust as oneself","He who scorns his own life is lord of it"],"terms":[{"t":"otium (leisure)","d":"Time reclaimed for oneself and for study, which Seneca urges Lucilius to guard."}]},{"i":253,"j":269,"loc":"Letters V–VII","title":"The philosopher's mean, shared knowledge, crowds","summary":"Letter V tells Lucilius to avoid showy austerity in dress and living: inwardly different, outwardly conforming, since philosophy promises fellow-feeling and calls for plain living, not penance; Hecato's saying links hope and fear. Letter VI reports Seneca's own transformation and his wish to share it, stressing that living example teaches more than precept, as with Zeno and Cleanthes or Socrates and his followers. Letter VII warns against crowds, especially the noon slaughter at the games, which sends Seneca home crueller. He advises withdrawing into oneself and seeking an audience of one, citing Democritus and Epicurus.","ideas":["Live according to Nature: simple but not squalid or eccentric","Hope and fear are chained together by anxiety about the future","Example and shared life teach better than lectures","The arena's butchery corrupts its spectators"],"terms":[{"t":"secundum naturam vivere (live according to Nature)","d":"The Stoic motto, which Seneca says forbids deliberate self-torment and squalor."}]},{"i":270,"j":295,"loc":"Letters VIII–IX","title":"Seclusion for others; the wise man's friendships","summary":"Letter VIII defends Seneca's retirement: he writes remedies for later generations, warns against Fortune's gifts as snares, and sets limits to bodily indulgence; he justifies quoting Epicurus as common property and cites Publilius and Lucilius. Letter IX takes up Epicurus' criticism of Stilbo: the Stoic sage feels troubles but overcomes them and is self-sufficient, yet still desires friends. He seeks them not for help in need, as Epicurus says, but to exercise friendship and to have someone to die for. Stilbo's reply to Demetrius, that he has all his goods with him, shows the sage needs nothing external for happiness.","ideas":["Writing for posterity is greater work than public business","The wise man can do without friends but does not wish to","Friendship for utility ends when the utility ends","The sage is self-sufficient for a happy life, not for mere existence"],"terms":[{"t":"apatheia / impatientia","d":"The 'insensibility' of the sage, which Seneca says Latin renders ambiguously and Stoics distinguish from not feeling at all."},{"t":"autarkeia (self-sufficiency)","d":"The sage's independence of externals for happiness, which still leaves room for friendship."}]},{"i":296,"j":312,"loc":"Letters X–XII","title":"Living to oneself, blushing, and old age","summary":"Letter X trusts Lucilius to keep his own company, recalling Crates' warning to a youth communing with a bad man, and urges prayers only for a sound mind and health that could be spoken aloud. Letter XI describes a young friend's blush and argues that wisdom cannot remove inborn bodily reactions like blushing or trembling; Epicurus advises keeping a revered man as an imagined witness. Letter XII begins with Seneca's crumbling country-house, old trees and aged slave Felicio, which reveal his own age. He praises old age's pleasures, cites Heraclitus on one day equalling all days, and urges living each day as if it completed life.","ideas":["Only a mind not foolish can safely be left alone","Natural bodily weaknesses can be tempered but not removed by wisdom","Choose a Cato or Laelius as a guardian of conduct","Each day should be lived as a finished life"],"terms":[{"t":"custos (guardian)","d":"An admired person kept in mind as a witness, per the Epicurean advice Seneca endorses."}]},{"i":313,"j":332,"loc":"Letters XIII–XIV","title":"Groundless fears and prudent withdrawal","summary":"Letter XIII argues that we suffer more in imagination than in fact: some things hurt more than, before, or when they should not; we should test rumours, weigh hope against fear, and recall that the feared may never come. It ends by mocking those always getting ready to live. Letter XIV grants the body care but not mastery, and sorts our fears into want, sickness, and violence from the powerful. The wise man avoids offending those in power, avoids hatred, envy and scorn, and takes refuge in a quiet philosophy; Seneca questions Cato's role in the civil war and notes that the sage judges intentions, not outcomes.","ideas":["Do not be unhappy before the crisis comes","Panic fear from rumour is the most ruinous","Steer clear of the powerful as a pilot avoids Charybdis","He who needs riches least enjoys them most"],"terms":[{"t":"Fortuna","d":"Chance personified, which may decide outcomes but cannot pass sentence on the wise man's choices."}]},{"i":333,"j":348,"loc":"Letters XV–XVII","title":"Body versus mind; philosophy as guide; riches","summary":"Letter XV mocks heavy athletic training and recommends brief exercises, then a quick return to exercising the mind, with sensible voice training. Letter XVI calls philosophy a matter of deeds that shapes the soul and steers life, and holds that whether Fate, God or Chance rules, we must still philosophize; natural desires have limits, those of opinion none. Letter XVII answers Lucilius' wish to secure his estate first: poverty is unburdened, simple living is voluntary poverty, and wisdom should come first, not as a supplement. Epicurus adds that riches merely change troubles, since the fault lies in the mind.","ideas":["Without philosophy the mind is sick, however strong the body","Philosophy teaches us to obey God cheerfully and endure Chance","Desires based on opinion have no stopping point","Do not postpone philosophy until you are rich"],"terms":[{"t":"opinio (opinion)","d":"False belief that breeds boundless desires, set against nature's few wants."}]},{"i":349,"j":365,"loc":"Letters XVIII–XIX","title":"Saturnalia, rehearsing poverty, and leaving office","summary":"Letter XVIII, written during the December Saturnalia, weighs joining the crowd's feasting in a different spirit, then urges Lucilius to set aside days of coarse food and dress to rehearse poverty, as Epicurus did. Such training shows peace of mind does not depend on Fortune; the closing maxim warns that ungoverned anger begets madness. Letter XIX presses Lucilius to retire from his procuratorship without parading or hiding it, since ambition never stops and success only adds fear. Maecenas' line about thunder on the highest peaks serves as warning, and Epicurus advises choosing companions before menus.","ideas":["Keep holiday without becoming one of the mob","Practise poverty in times of ease so Fortune cannot surprise you","Desires succeed one another like a chain of causes","Benefits must be placed with judgment, not scattered"],"terms":[{"t":"Saturnalia","d":"The December festival of license during which Seneca writes and discusses whether to join in."}]},{"i":366,"j":385,"loc":"Letters XX–XXI","title":"Deeds matching words; renown through letters","summary":"Letter XX insists that philosophy teaches action, not speech: a man should live by one standard, his words and deeds in accord. Wisdom is always wanting and refusing the same things; poverty reveals true friends, and Demetrius' bare living makes him a witness to truth, though what matters is whether a man chooses poverty freely. Letter XXI says Lucilius' real obstacle is himself and his attachment to his present lustre. As Epicurus' letters immortalized Idomeneus and Cicero's Atticus, Seneca's will preserve Lucilius; the Garden's welcome of barley and water illustrates pleasure that quenches desire.","ideas":["The highest proof of wisdom is consistency of word and deed","Rehearsed poverty becomes easy, even pleasant","Inner light outlasts borrowed brightness of office","To enrich someone, subtract from his desires"],"terms":[{"t":"constantia (consistency)","d":"Being the same in all conditions, which Seneca makes the mark of wisdom."}]},{"i":386,"j":399,"loc":"Letters XXII–XXIII","title":"No half-measures; the stern joy of philosophy","summary":"Letter XXII says the timing of Lucilius' escape from business must be judged on the spot, but he should loosen the knot gently and, if needed, cut it, and pursue nothing further. Epicurus and the Stoics alike counsel seasonable withdrawal; men cling to the slavery they curse, and no one can swim ashore with his baggage. Epicurus' saying that all leave life as if just born leads Seneca to note we die worse than we were born. Letter XXIII teaches Lucilius to learn real joy, a serious inner joy from good conscience and right action, not from external glitter; it ends on those who are always beginning to live.","ideas":["Many men hold fast to their slavery","Withdraw from business by despising its rewards","Real joy is inward, solid and stern","Decide what you wish and abide by it"],"terms":[{"t":"gaudium (joy)","d":"The steady inner gladness from virtue, contrasted with laughter and shallow pleasure."}]},{"i":400,"j":414,"loc":"Letter XXIV","title":"On despising death: exemplars and arguments","summary":"Facing Lucilius' anxiety over a lawsuit, Seneca advises assuming the worst will happen and measuring it, then lists those who scorned exile, prison, fire and death: Rutilius, Metellus, Socrates, Mucius, Cato reading Plato before his suicide, and Scipio. He urges stripping the masks from things, addressing Death and Pain directly as slight or short. He argues that death either annihilates or frees us, that we die daily, quoting Lucilius' own verse, and adds Epicurean sayings against both craving and fleeing death. The letter ends warning against the lust for death and against weariness with life's repeating round.","ideas":["Imagine the feared outcome as certain and measure it","Strip the mask from things as from frightening faces","We die every day; the last hour only completes it","Neither love nor hate life overmuch"],"terms":[{"t":"libido moriendi (lust for death)","d":"An unreflecting urge toward death that Seneca warns can seize even noble men."}]},{"i":415,"j":427,"loc":"Letters XXV–XXVII","title":"Reforming friends, readiness for death, abiding good","summary":"Letter XXV discusses two friends, one to be corrected and one, a forty-year-old, to be handled carefully; it urges Lucilius to return to nature's needs and, with Epicurus, to act as if watched by a good man. Letter XXVI has Seneca, now worn by age but strong in mind, resolving that death will judge his progress, and glossing Epicurus' 'think on death' as thinking on freedom. Letter XXVII presents Seneca as a fellow patient, urging that faults die before oneself and that only virtue gives lasting joy. The rich Calvisius Sabinus, who bought slaves to know poets for him, shows a sound mind cannot be bought.","ideas":["Shame is grounds for hope of reform","Death alone tests whether one's brave words are real","He who has learned to die has unlearned slavery","Virtue is the only good that abides"],"terms":[{"t":"meditatio mortis (thinking on death)","d":"Rehearsing death so that the love of life cannot bind one, which Seneca equates with freedom."}]},{"i":428,"j":440,"loc":"Letters XXVIII–XXIX","title":"Travel cannot cure the soul; Marcellinus","summary":"Letter XXVIII tells Lucilius that travel fails because he takes himself along; a change of soul, not climate, is needed, and the whole world is one's country. The wise man endures turmoil but prefers peace, and Epicurus' saying that knowing one's sin begins salvation calls for self-accusation. Letter XXIX reports on Marcellinus, who avoids hearing the truth; Seneca rejects indiscriminate Cynic advice, holding that wisdom is an art that chooses its patients. He plans to confront Marcellinus despite his mocking wit, and closes with Epicurus on not catering to the crowd.","ideas":["You flee along with yourself","Slavery has no plural: one who scorns it is free","Wisdom, as an art, should aim and not scatter advice","Weigh men's judgments rather than count them"],"terms":[{"t":"Cynics","d":"Diogenes' followers, whose undiscriminating free speech Seneca judges ineffective."}]},{"i":441,"j":451,"loc":"Letter XXX","title":"Aufidius Bassus faces death in old age","summary":"Seneca describes the aged Aufidius Bassus, whose body is collapsing like a leaking ship while his mind stays active and cheerful. Bassus argues that fear of death is the dying man's fault, that one cannot feel the removal of feeling, and that death follows old age as old age follows youth. Seneca finds these familiar precepts weightier from a man near death, and holds that steady courage before an approaching end is rarer than courage at the moment. Bassus adds Epicurean comfort that great pain is short, and Seneca concludes we fear not death but the thought of death.","ideas":["Old age alone grants no reprieve, yet is the gentlest end","Death is equal and fixed for all, hence nothing to resent","We fear the thought of death, not death itself","Always think on death in order never to fear it"],"terms":[]},{"i":452,"j":463,"loc":"Letters XXXI–XXXII","title":"Siren songs and hastening toward progress","summary":"Seneca praises Lucilius for spurning what the crowd approves and urges him to stop his ears more tightly than Ulysses did, since the siren voices of well-wishers come from every side. The only good is trust in oneself, grounded in knowledge of things; toil is indifferent, but scorning toil and striving toward the honourable is praiseworthy. Wealth, rank and beauty cannot make a man equal to God; only an upright soul, a god dwelling as a guest in the body, can, and it may arise in a knight, a freedman or a slave. Letter XXXII warns that the crowd may slow Lucilius' progress, that life is cut short by ever-fresh beginnings, and prays that he may round out his life before death and need no added years.","ideas":["The prayers of loved ones ask for things that are not truly good","Good is what is joined with virtue; knowledge of things is the good","The good soul is a god lodged in the body, open to any social rank","A completed life is not made happier by being longer"],"terms":[{"t":"sapiens (the wise man)","d":"Seneca's ideal of the person whose soul is upright and self-reliant, the associate rather than the suppliant of the gods."}]},{"i":464,"j":478,"loc":"Letters XXXIII–XXXV","title":"Against maxims; the pupil and true friendship","summary":"Asked for more quoted maxims, Seneca replies that Stoic writings are full of strength throughout, unlike Epicurus, whose sayings stand out because they are rare and all credited to one master. Epitomes and memorised sayings suit children; a grown man must speak from his own stock, for remembering differs from knowing, and past thinkers are guides, not masters, with truth still open to discovery. Letter XXXIV rejoices that Lucilius, Seneca's own handiwork, now runs the race himself, and holds that the will to be good is most of goodness. Letter XXXV distinguishes loving from being a friend and urges Lucilius to perfect himself, judging progress by whether he wants the same things today as yesterday.","ideas":["Stoics are not subjects of a despot; each claims his own freedom","Remembering another's words is not the same as knowing","The will to become good is the larger part of goodness","Consistency of desire is the test of progress"],"terms":[{"t":"chria","d":"A short saying learned by heart in elementary teaching, fit for the young but not for one well advanced."}]},{"i":479,"j":496,"loc":"Letters XXXVI–XXXIX","title":"Retirement, the soldier's oath, and noble aspiration","summary":"Seneca tells Lucilius to encourage a friend who has retired from public honours to despise his critics, since prosperity is turbulent and only character lies outside fortune's reach. The friend should above all learn contempt of death: death harms no one, and like the stars and seasons things only change and return. Letter XXXVII likens the pledge to virtue to a gladiator's oath, endured willingly, and names philosophy as the one road to freedom from the passions. Letter XXXVIII prefers quiet conversation, whose few words grow like seeds, to loud lectures, and Letter XXXIX, sending notes as requested, says a noble soul is roused by great examples and that excess ruins the soul as rich soil flattens grain.","ideas":["Fortune has no jurisdiction over character","Contempt of death is the defence against every weapon","Wisdom is the only real freedom; put yourself under reason","Pleasures once superfluous become indispensable, and men become their slaves"],"terms":[{"t":"otium (retirement, leisure)","d":"Withdrawal from public career, which Seneca defends when it is used for virtue and study."}]},{"i":497,"j":511,"loc":"Letters XL–XLI","title":"Measured speech and the god within","summary":"Thanking Lucilius for frequent letters, Seneca criticises the philosopher Serapio's torrent of words: a philosopher's speech, like his life, should be composed, plain and controlled, since remedies for the mind must sink in. He compares the speed of Haterius with the slowness of Vinicius and Cicero, notes national differences in style, and ends by bidding Lucilius be slow of speech. Letter XLI holds that one need not pray for a sound mind, because God is within us: a holy spirit dwells in us and watches our deeds. Groves, caves and springs suggest divinity, and so does a soul unshaken by danger, which stays tied to its heavenly source like a sunbeam; man's own good is reason perfected, living by his nature.","ideas":["Speech that cannot govern itself cannot govern others","A holy spirit dwells within each person as guardian","Praise only what is a man's own, not his gilded trappings","Man's highest good is reason brought to perfection"],"terms":[{"t":"sacer spiritus (holy spirit)","d":"The divine presence Seneca places within the good person, marking good and bad deeds."},{"t":"animal rationale (reasoning animal)","d":"Seneca's definition of man, whose proper excellence is perfected reason."}]},{"i":512,"j":524,"loc":"Letters XLII–XLIV","title":"Values, fame and true nobility","summary":"Seneca doubts that Lucilius' friend is good, since a truly good man is as rare as the phoenix and many men's vices merely lack the power to show themselves. We should weigh the real price of what we seek, for things gained with anxiety, danger, freedom and time cost us most, and losses trouble us only through our notion of loss. Letter XLIII observes that greatness is relative, that Lucilius is watched in his province, and that a good conscience can live with an open door. Letter XLIV says philosophy ignores pedigree, citing Socrates, Cleanthes and Plato: the soul alone makes one noble, and men fail at happiness by mistaking its means for happiness itself.","ideas":["Many vices are numbed only by lack of opportunity, like a cold snake","What costs us ourselves is the dearest purchase","A good conscience welcomes witnesses; a bad one is troubled alone","The well-born man is the one fitted by nature for virtue"],"terms":[]},{"i":525,"j":537,"loc":"Letters XLV–XLVI","title":"Against sophistical argument; Lucilius' book","summary":"Lucilius lacks books, but Seneca says a limited reading list benefits more than variety. He asks that his own works be read as those of one still seeking the truth, bound to no master, and faults earlier thinkers for losing time on verbal quibbles. The real need is to distinguish things, such as flattery from friendship and vice from virtue, not words; tricks like the horns puzzle or the liar fallacy do no good. He sketches the truly happy man, notes that the necessary is not always good, and attacks people who only prepare to live. Letter XLVI praises a new book by Lucilius for its even, vigorous style and promises a fuller verdict after rereading.","ideas":["It is things, not words, that lead us astray","Vices creep in under the names of virtues","The necessary is not thereby a good","Those who postpone everything do not live but prepare to live"],"terms":[{"t":"mentiens (the liar fallacy)","d":"A famous logical paradox about a speaker who says he is lying, which Seneca dismisses as useless."}]},{"i":538,"j":551,"loc":"Letter XLVII","title":"On master and slave","summary":"Seneca approves of Lucilius living on friendly terms with his slaves, who are men, comrades and fellow-slaves under Fortune. He describes the cruelty of banquets where slaves stand silent and are made to carve, serve wine in boyish dress or judge the guests, and notes that masters make slaves into enemies. Fortune reverses ranks, as with Callistus and his former master and the men ruined in Marius' day, so one should treat inferiors as one would be treated by one's betters. Slaves should be valued by character and invited to the table, since everyone is a slave to lust, greed or fear, and masters should seek respect, which is love, rather than fear.","ideas":["Slaves share the same origin, sky and death as free men","Treat your inferior as you would wish your superior to treat you","Value men by character, which they make, not duties, which chance assigns","Respect and love cannot be mixed with fear"],"terms":[{"t":"paterfamilias / familiares","d":"The ancestral titles of father and members of the household, which Seneca says removed insult from slavery."}]},{"i":552,"j":571,"loc":"Letters XLVIII–XLIX","title":"Quibbling and the shortness of life","summary":"Seneca says friendship makes a full partnership of interests: one must live for one's neighbour to live for oneself. He mocks dialecticians who analyse the meanings of words like 'friend' and offer syllogisms such as the mouse that is a syllable, since philosophy is counsel for people in fear, poverty and danger, and promises to make us equal to God. Letter XLIX, written in Campania, recalls how swiftly time has passed since Seneca studied with Sotion: a lifetime is less than a point. With death pressing like a besieging army, he has no time for sophisms and asks to be taught courage, the right use of life, and justice and duty in simple language.","ideas":["Friendship makes a partnership in all interests","Logic-chopping weakens a noble spirit and helps no one","Life is a mere point; time flies fastest in retrospect","The good of life depends on its use, not its length"],"terms":[{"t":"dialectica (dialectic)","d":"Formal logic, which Seneca would allow only a glance from the threshold so as not to be deceived."}]},{"i":572,"j":584,"loc":"Letters L–LI","title":"Blindness to our faults; Baiae and luxury","summary":"Seneca tells of Harpaste, a household clown who went blind but thinks her rooms are dark, as a picture of how we blame place and circumstance for vices that are within us. Since no one knows he is ill, cure is hard, yet the soul is more pliable than bent timber, learning virtue means unlearning vice, and virtue once gained is not lost. Letter LI explains that he left Baiae after one day, because it is a resort of vice, and argues that one should choose places healthy for character. Hannibal was softened by Campania; the philosopher fights a war with pleasure and Fortune, and freedom means being a slave to nothing.","ideas":["The evil that afflicts us is inside, not in places or times","Virtue accords with nature; vice is foreign and can be driven out","Surroundings that are too pleasant weaken the spirit","Freedom is compelling Fortune to meet us on equal terms"],"terms":[{"t":"libertas (freedom)","d":"For Seneca, not being a slave to any circumstance, constraint or chance."}]},{"i":585,"j":603,"loc":"Letters LII–LIII","title":"Choosing teachers; seasickness and hidden faults","summary":"Noting how we veer between plans, Seneca reports Epicurus' three classes of men: those who reach truth unaided, those who follow a guide like Metrodorus, and those who must be driven like Hermarchus, the last deserving respect for working with harder material. He advises choosing living teachers who prove their words by their lives, or the ancients, and condemns lecture halls where philosophy courts applause. Letter LIII tells how seasickness drove him to leap into the sea off Puteoli, and from this he reflects that bodily ills reveal themselves while the soul's diseases hide more deeply the worse they are. Only philosophy can wake us, and she must be given all our time, not what is left over.","ideas":["Some reach wisdom unaided, some need a guide, some must be forced","Choose a guide admired more for his acts than his words","To confess a fault, like recounting a dream, requires being awake","The sage has a man's weakness and a god's serenity"],"terms":[]},{"i":604,"j":614,"loc":"Letters LIV–LV","title":"Asthma, death, and Vatia's villa","summary":"Seneca describes his attacks of shortness of breath, which physicians call practising how to die, and tells how he comforted himself during them: death is non-existence, which he already knew before birth, like a lamp before it was lit. The wise man is never thrust out, for he wills what necessity would force. Letter LV recounts a litter ride past the villa of Servilius Vatia, admired for surviving the dangers of the Sejanus years by retiring. Seneca says Vatia knew how to hide, not how to live, since leisure differs from idleness; the place matters less than the mind, and absent friends can be kept present in thought.","ideas":["Death both precedes and follows life; both are non-existence","The wise man does nothing unwillingly","Hiding from affairs is not the same as living for oneself","A friend held in the spirit is never absent"],"terms":[{"t":"suspirium (shortness of breath)","d":"Seneca's chronic breathing ailment, which he treats as a rehearsal for death."}]},{"i":615,"j":629,"loc":"Letters LVI–LVII","title":"Noise, inner quiet, and the Naples tunnel","summary":"Lodging over a bath-house, Seneca lists its din of grunting athletes, shouting vendors and splashing swimmers, and claims he can ignore it, since true quiet depends on peace within, not a silent neighbourhood. Hidden vices like ambition and luxury return in retirement, and the anxious man, like Vergil's hero fearing for his child and load, starts at every sound; Seneca will still move house. Letter LVII describes the dark, dusty tunnel to Naples, which gave him a thrill that is natural rather than fear, and even a wise man feels such things. He rejects the view of some Stoics that a crushed soul is scattered, arguing that its subtle substance escapes.","ideas":["No quiet neighbourhood helps if the emotions are in uproar","Vices concealed behind a show of soundness do most harm","Some involuntary reactions are nature, not fear","The soul, subtler than fire, cannot be crushed"],"terms":[{"t":"tranquillitas (tranquillity)","d":"The state reached by an unperverted mind at rest, which outward silence alone cannot supply."}]},{"i":630,"j":641,"loc":"Letter LVIII, 1–15","title":"Latin's poverty and the highest genus","summary":"Prompted by a discussion of Plato, Seneca laments the poverty of Latin, citing obsolete words from Vergil, and asks leave to use essentia for the Greek ousia, with Cicero and Fabianus as authority. He finds no single Latin word for on and must render it 'that which exists'. He then explains genus and species: reasoning back from man, horse and dog to animal, living things and substance, he reaches 'that which exists' as the highest genus, divided into things with and without substance. Some Stoics place a still prior genus, the 'something', to include things that do not exist, such as centaurs and giants.","ideas":["Latin lacks words for key Greek philosophical terms","Genus and species are reached by reasoning back from particulars","'That which exists' is the first and most general genus","Some Stoics set 'something' above it to cover non-existents"],"terms":[{"t":"essentia / ousia","d":"Seneca's Latin coinage, backed by Cicero, for the Greek word for being or substance."},{"t":"quod est (that which exists)","d":"Seneca's rendering of Greek on, used as the highest genus in his classification."}]},{"i":642,"j":652,"loc":"Letter LVIII, 16–37","title":"Plato's six senses of being; old age","summary":"Seneca sets out the six ways in which, by a learned friend's account, Plato speaks of what exists: what is grasped by thought, the pre-eminent being who is God, the ideas as eternal patterns, the form embodied in a work, ordinary things, and quasi-existents like void and time. Using a painter of Vergil and a statue, he distinguishes the idea as pattern from the form in the work, and with Heraclitus' river shows that bodies are always in flux. The moral use is to despise fleeting things, look to the everlasting and God, and prolong life by frugality, as Plato did, dying at eighty-one. He ends by saying he will leave life if old age destroys his mind or pain becomes permanent.","ideas":["Plato's ideas are eternal patterns of what nature makes","The idea lies outside and prior to the work; the form is in it","We change daily, so we already die continually","Frugal living can prolong life; departure is justified if the mind fails"],"terms":[{"t":"idea","d":"In Seneca's account of Plato, the everlasting pattern of the things nature creates."},{"t":"forma (form)","d":"The shape taken from the pattern and embodied in the finished work."}]},{"i":653,"j":674,"loc":"Letters LIX–LXII","title":"Joy versus pleasure; prayers and death","summary":"Seneca distinguishes Stoic joy, an elation of a spirit trusting its own goods and never turning to its opposite, from pleasure, which rests on a spurious good. He praises Lucilius' controlled style and cites Sextius' image of the wise man as an army in hollow square. Folly holds us because we fight it weakly and accept flattery, unlike Alexander, whose wound proved him mortal; true joy comes only from virtue and is like the calm above the moon. Letter LX condemns the prayers of our families and greed beyond nature's needs; LXI resolves to live each day as complete and to die gladly; LXII says he is free of business and keeps company with the best, such as Demetrius.","ideas":["Only the wise know joy; pleasure is unstable and borrowed","The wise man's defences face every side","Willing what circumstances demand removes the sting of necessity","The shortest road to riches is to despise them"],"terms":[{"t":"gaudium (joy)","d":"In Stoic usage a lasting elation of the wise, distinct from pleasure (voluptas)."}]},{"i":675,"j":689,"loc":"Letters LXIII–LXIV","title":"Grief for friends and reverence for teachers","summary":"On the death of Lucilius' friend Flaccus, Seneca allows tears but not wailing, saying excessive grief is often display and self-seeking. He reports Attalus' view that memory of dead friends is pleasant with a touch of bitterness, but finds such memory simply sweet, and urges Lucilius to enjoy friends while he has them and to replace a lost friend. He confesses he grieved too much for Annaeus Serenus because he never expected the younger man to die first. Letter LXIV describes a dinner where a book of Quintus Sextius was read, praises its strength, and calls on us to increase the inherited wisdom of the ancients and to honour them like magistrates.","ideas":["Weep but do not wail; much mourning is a parade","Have friends as if you will lose them; lose them as if you still have them","Mortality follows no fixed order of age","Later thinkers must apply and add to the ancients' remedies"],"terms":[]},{"i":690,"j":698,"loc":"Letter LXV","title":"On the first cause","summary":"Recovering from illness, Seneca reports a debate among friends and makes Lucilius referee. The Stoics posit two principles, matter and cause, which is reason, and recognise a single cause, the maker; Aristotle names four causes (matter, maker, form, purpose), using a bronze statue as example, and Plato adds the idea or pattern, which God holds in his mind, and says God made the world from goodness. Seneca objects that their list is too long or too short, since time, place and motion would also count, and that the first cause must be simple: creative reason, God. He defends such inquiry as freeing the soul from the body's bondage and ends by saying God relates to matter as the soul to the body, so death is either an end or a change.","ideas":["Stoics: matter and a single cause, reason or God","Aristotle's four causes and Plato's fifth, the idea","Contemplating the universe frees the soul from the body","Death is either ending or transformation, neither to be feared"],"terms":[{"t":"causa (cause)","d":"For the Stoics the active, creative reason that shapes passive matter."},{"t":"idos (eidos, form)","d":"Aristotle's formal cause, the shape stamped upon a work."}]},{"i":699,"j":711,"loc":"Letter LXVI 1–35","title":"Claranus and the equality of all goods","summary":"Seneca describes meeting his old school-mate Claranus, a great soul in a frail body, as proof that virtue needs no fair exterior. Their first conversation asks how goods can be equal when the Stoics rank them in three kinds: primary goods like joy, goods shown in hardship like endurance of illness, and goods of bearing like a modest gait. Seneca argues that virtue, as right reason and a portion of the divine, admits no increase, so joy and brave endurance of torture are equal goods, differing only in their material. He answers the objection from Epicurus's bull of Phalaris and insists that we love good men and good acts for their virtue, not their circumstances.","ideas":["A strong and happy mind can dwell in an ugly, feeble body","Virtue cannot grow or shrink, since what is perfect admits no addition","Joy and endurance differ in circumstance, which is indifferent, but not in virtue","Virtue cares for all its works like a parent, most for those in trouble"],"terms":[{"t":"recta ratio (right reason)","d":"Seneca's definition of virtue; since right reason is single, all virtues and all right actions are equal."},{"t":"indifferentia (indifferent things)","d":"Circumstances such as pleasure or pain that are neither good nor evil and do not alter the virtue shown in them."}]},{"i":712,"j":717,"loc":"Letter LXVI 36–53","title":"Primary, secondary and indifferent goods; Mucius","summary":"Seneca restates the three classes of goods: primary goods according to nature, secondary goods arising in adversity, and things indifferent such as posture. Goods can concern material contrary to nature, but a good itself never is, since reason copies nature. Against the objector who thinks untroubled peace and health are greater than recovered ones, he compares goods to votes for one motion and to death, which is equal however it comes. He cites Epicurus, who praised a body free of pain and a calm soul and called his agonised last day happy. He closes by boldly preferring hard-won goods, with Mucius's burnt hand as his example.","ideas":["Reason is the imitation of nature; the highest good is living as nature wills","Every death is equal in its end, as every good is equal in being good","Epicurus too recognised goods one would rather not experience","If goods differed, those won through hardship would rank higher"],"terms":[{"t":"secundum naturam (according to nature)","d":"The standard by which goods are measured; all goods agree with nature equally even when their material does not."}]},{"i":718,"j":727,"loc":"Letter LXVII","title":"Whether brave endurance is desirable","summary":"Writing from a chilly spring and an old man's bed, Seneca takes up Lucilius's question whether every good is desirable, given that no one prays for torture or gout. He distinguishes: hardships are not desirable, but the virtue that bears them is. Against some Stoics who hold endurance not to be desirable, he argues that every good is desirable and endurance under torture is part of bravery. A prayer for an honourable life implicitly includes Regulus's chest, Cato's wound, Rutilius's exile and Socrates's poison. He cites Demetrius on the easy life as a Dead Sea and Attalus on preferring Fortune's camp, and refuses Epicurus's word pleasant for such acts.","ideas":["What is desired is not hardship but the virtue that endures it","All virtues act together, as a council, in any brave endurance","A life untested by Fortune is a flat calm, not tranquillity","Virtue is worshipped with sweat and blood, not incense"],"terms":[{"t":"patientia (endurance)","d":"The brave bearing of pain, which Seneca treats as a branch of bravery and hence a desirable good."}]},{"i":728,"j":736,"loc":"Letters LXVIII–LXIX","title":"Hidden retirement and staying in one place","summary":"Seneca approves Lucilius's plan to retire but urges him to conceal it rather than advertise himself as a philosopher, since hidden things draw curiosity. The Stoic sage, whose commonwealth is the universe, is never more active than in contemplating things divine and human. In leisure one should speak ill of oneself and treat one's weakest part; Seneca calls himself a sick man curing his own sores, not a physician. Retirement is for nobler work than courting the powerful, and old age suits the cure of passions. Letter LXIX warns against restless travel, which revives old cravings, and advises practising how to welcome or invite death.","ideas":["To boast of one's retirement is to collect a crowd","The wise man's true field of public life is the whole universe","Seneca presents himself as a patient, not a teacher","Constant moving keeps old desires alive; no one dies except on his own day"],"terms":[{"t":"otium (leisure, retirement)","d":"Withdrawal from public affairs, which Seneca commends only when used for self-examination and philosophy."}]},{"i":737,"j":751,"loc":"Letter LXX","title":"On the proper time to slip the cable","summary":"Revisiting Pompeii, Seneca compares life to a voyage whose end is a harbour, not a reef. Since living well, not mere living, is the good, the wise man lives as long as he ought and may free himself when life turns ill. He weighs cases: the caged Rhodian who clung to hope, Socrates who awaited the poison to obey the laws, and Libo, advised by Scribonia. No general rule settles whether to anticipate a death imposed by others, but he rejects those who call self-killing forbidden, since the eternal law gave many exits. He ends with gladiators and captives who found means to die, arguing the foulest death beats the fairest slavery.","ideas":["Quality of life, not its length, is what matters","Nature gives one entrance into life but many exits","Choose one's death as one chooses a ship or a house","Even the humblest captives show that only the will postpones death"],"terms":[{"t":"libertas (freedom)","d":"Here the freedom to leave life, which Seneca says no one can be denied."}]},{"i":752,"j":760,"loc":"Letter LXXI 1–20","title":"The supreme good as the honourable","summary":"Since advice arrives too late across the sea, Seneca offers a method: judge every choice by the Supreme Good, the aim of one's whole life. That good is simply the honourable, and it is the only good. He cites Socrates on being happy though despised, and Cato, whose virtue was equal whether he won or lost the praetorship or the civil war. All things, even the universe, are born, grow and are resolved into elements, so the wise man accepts change and death calmly. Cato's honourable death was as much a good as his life; against the earlier Academics, Seneca holds the tortured good man fully happy.","ideas":["Without a fixed aim, no wind is the right wind","The honourable is the only good; other goods are alloyed","The world itself will perish and be remade by God's craftsmanship","Virtue, like a carpenter's rule, cannot be bent or made straighter"],"terms":[{"t":"summum bonum (Supreme Good)","d":"The final aim of life, which Seneca identifies with the honourable alone."},{"t":"honestum (the honourable)","d":"Moral goodness, the one true good on the Stoic view."}]},{"i":761,"j":765,"loc":"Letter LXXI 21–37","title":"Torture, the sage, and those still progressing","summary":"Seneca defends the claim that reclining on the rack honourably is a good while banqueting shamefully is an evil, since virtue, not material, decides. Critics judge virtue by their own softness, as a straight stick looks bent in water. The sage feels pain in his irrational part but his rational part, where the Supreme Good resides, stands firm. Seneca distinguishes the sage from the learner, confessing his own principles are not yet steeped in him like dyed wool. He sums the doctrine in a sentence and urges continued progress, ending with victory over greed, ambition and fear of death.","ideas":["Hardships seem hard because we are soft","The wise man trembles and pales but his mind is not conquered","Doctrine must permeate the soul, not merely colour it","Much of progress is the will to progress"],"terms":[{"t":"proficiens (one making progress)","d":"The learner not yet wise, who still wavers and can slip back."}]},{"i":766,"j":783,"loc":"Letters LXXII–LXXIII","title":"Philosophy against business; philosophers and rulers","summary":"In Letter LXXII Seneca defers a question he has half forgotten, but warns that philosophy must not wait for leisure, since interrupted study falls back to the start. The wise man's joy is woven from within and cannot be broken, whereas the learner is like a convalescent who may relapse; Attalus compares people to dogs snapping at Fortune's scraps. Letter LXXIII rejects the idea that philosophers despise rulers: those who use peace for right living owe the ruler most, as a rich merchant owes Neptune more. Peace and liberty belong wholly to each person. He ends with Sextius's view that the good man equals Jupiter and that God enters men as divine seeds.","ideas":["Business must be shut out early or it multiplies","Three classes: the wise, the progressing, and those near port","The philosopher is most grateful for the peace a ruler secures","Divine seeds in the soul grow good or bad according to the husbandman"],"terms":[{"t":"occupationes (business, engagements)","d":"The affairs that crowd out study, which Seneca calls philosophy's enemy."}]},{"i":784,"j":795,"loc":"Letter LXXIV 1–21","title":"Fortune's dole and the defence of reason","summary":"Seneca agrees that the happy life depends on holding the honourable the only good, since whoever counts other things good falls into Fortune's power. He lists the anxieties of parents, lovers, candidates and those fearing death, and pictures Fortune scattering gifts at a festival where the scramble brings only loss; the sensible man leaves the theatre. If external things were goods, God would be worse off than man and beasts better off than both. Such things should be called advantages or preferred things, held loosely. The only sure defence is reason and acceptance of the world's order.","ideas":["Believing externals good puts one under Fortune's control","Fortune's gifts are torn apart by those who grab them","God and the animals show that pleasures and riches are not goods","Walls fail against Fortune; the inner defence never falls"],"terms":[{"t":"producta / commoda (preferred things, advantages)","d":"External things that may be chosen but are not true goods and lie outside the self."}]},{"i":796,"j":800,"loc":"Letter LXXIV 22–34","title":"Losing children, friends and country","summary":"Seneca answers the objection that, if good children and country are goods, the sage must be disturbed by losing them. The usual Stoic reply is that such loss brings no evil in exchange, and that virtue, the source that made them good, fills the gap. Virtue's measure does not change with numbers or years, as a circle's shape is the same whatever its size. His own further answer is that the wise man bears others' deaths as he awaits his own, keeping virtue's consistency. Involuntary pallor or trembling is not an evil, and he warns against suffering in advance from future or past ills.","ideas":["A good can be lost only by turning bad, which virtue cannot","An honourable life of one day is as honourable as one of a hundred years","The sage may show bodily signs of feeling without judging them evils","Past and future are absent; pain comes only from what is felt"],"terms":[]},{"i":801,"j":809,"loc":"Letter LXXV","title":"Plain style and the stages of progress","summary":"Answering complaints about his careless letters, Seneca says they should read like conversation: say what we feel and feel what we say, for speech should fit life and aim to help, not please. A sick man wants a cure, not an eloquent physician. He then sets out the three classes of those making progress: those near wisdom who have shed passions and vices but are not yet sure of it, those who have shed great ills but may slip back, and those free of some vices but not others. He distinguishes chronic diseases of the mind from passing passions. The reward is freedom: fearing neither men nor gods and being master of oneself.","ideas":["Words should display facts rather than themselves","Philosophy must be practised, not merely memorised","Even the third class is a real gain amid general vice","True freedom is power over oneself"],"terms":[{"t":"morbus (disease of the mind)","d":"A hardened, chronic vice such as greed, a persistent misjudgment of what is desirable."},{"t":"affectus / pathos (passion)","d":"A sudden, vehement impulse of the spirit which, repeated, can harden into disease."}]},{"i":810,"j":816,"loc":"Letter LXXVI 1–19","title":"Learning in old age; reason as man's good","summary":"Seneca confesses that, old as he is, he is attending the philosopher Metronax's lectures, and contrasts the crowded Neapolitan theatre with the empty hall where the good man is discussed. Virtue does not come by chance, so Lucilius must hasten. He then proves more fully that the honourable is the only good: each thing is judged by its peculiar quality, as a vine by its wine or a ship by its soundness. Man's peculiar quality is reason, and perfected reason is virtue. We approve or disapprove a man for this alone, whatever else he has, and whatever strengthens the soul is good.","ideas":["Keep learning how to live as long as you live","Each thing is valued by the quality for which it exists","Reason alone is peculiar to man; perfected, it is virtue","Wisdom never slips back into folly"],"terms":[{"t":"ratio perfecta (perfect reason)","d":"Reason brought to completion, identified with virtue and the honourable."}]},{"i":817,"j":826,"loc":"Letter LXXVI 20–35","title":"Why only the honourable is good","summary":"Seneca adds arguments: passion can make men scorn pain and death for a moment, so lasting virtue can do far more, showing such things are neither good nor evil. Any good besides the honourable would undermine the virtues and piety toward the gods, and would make man happier than gods and beasts happy. Readiness to die for one's country shows that one holds no other good. Those in purple are like actors in costume; a man must be judged stripped of his trappings, even of his body. He ends on forethought: the wise man rehearses all ills, so none comes as new.","ideas":["The soul freed from the body should be better off, not worse","We measure the pedestal along with the man","The good man acts rightly whatever thanks or memory follow","Evils pondered beforehand strike gently"],"terms":[{"t":"praemeditatio (forethought of ills)","d":"Rehearsing possible misfortunes in advance so that they lose their novelty and force."}]},{"i":827,"j":840,"loc":"Letter LXXVII","title":"Marcellinus's death and the fear of dying","summary":"Watching the Alexandrian mail-boats arrive at Puteoli, Seneca feels no hurry for news, since life is complete at whatever point it ends honourably. He tells how Tullius Marcellinus, troubled by a long illness, consulted friends; a Stoic told him living is unimportant but dying well matters, reassured his slaves, and Marcellinus fasted and died gently in a warm bath. Seneca argues that grieving at not living a thousand years hence is as foolish as not having lived a thousand years before, and fate is fixed. He cites the Spartan boy who refused slavery and Gaius Caesar's retort to a prisoner. Life is like a play: the acting matters, not the length.","ideas":["A life ended nobly is a whole life","Future non-existence is no worse than past non-existence","Clinging to exhausted pleasures is the real reason many fear death","Dying is also one of life's duties"],"terms":[{"t":"fatum (fate)","d":"The unbreakable sequence binding all things, which prayers cannot alter."}]},{"i":841,"j":855,"loc":"Letter LXXVIII","title":"Philosophy as a cure for illness","summary":"Seneca sympathises with Lucilius's catarrh and recalls his own youthful illness, when only the thought of his father kept him from ending his life; philosophy and friends restored him. Disease has three burdens: fear of death, pain and loss of pleasures. He answers each: death comes from being alive, not ill; severe pain is short or numbing; lost appetites fade. Pain depends greatly on opinion, so one should not dwell on past suffering or dread future pain. Illness leaves room for virtue and mental pleasures, and Posidonius is cited on a day among the learned.","ideas":["Sometimes it is brave even to live","Pain is either endurable or brief","Everything depends on opinion; we suffer as we judge","One can show bravery wrapped in bedclothes"],"terms":[{"t":"opinio (opinion)","d":"Judgment about things, which Seneca says adds most of what makes pain and loss hard."}]},{"i":856,"j":862,"loc":"Letter LXXIX","title":"Aetna, Charybdis, and the shadow of fame","summary":"Seneca asks Lucilius, touring Sicily, to report on Charybdis and to climb Aetna, discussing whether the mountain is shrinking, and urges him to write on Aetna though Vergil and Ovid already have. Later writers are not forestalled, but in wisdom all who reach the top are equal, since virtue cannot rise further. Our souls rejoice only when they regain the light of their heavenly origin. Fame is virtue's shadow, sometimes late: Democritus, Socrates, Cato, Rutilius and Epicurus won renown only later. Virtue rewards a man in life and after, provided he wears no mask.","ideas":["Literary themes are common property and grow with each writer","Among the wise there is equality, as nothing exceeds the summit","The soul is drawn upward by its origin","Fame follows virtue like a shadow, often after death"],"terms":[]},{"i":863,"j":873,"loc":"Letter LXXX","title":"Athletes, freedom, and the actor's mask","summary":"Freed by the crowds at a boxing match, Seneca reflects that many train their bodies but few their minds, though the mind could be toughened against Fortune at no cost. To become good one need only wish it, and freedom cannot be bought but must be given to oneself, starting with release from fear of death and of poverty. The poor laugh more genuinely than the rich, whose happiness is feigned. He compares the powerful to actors who play kings yet earn a slave's ration. We strip horses and slaves to judge them, and should do the same with men and ourselves.","ideas":["Seneca follows predecessors but feels free to alter and reject","Virtue needs no equipment or expense, only the will","Worldly happiness is put on like a mask","Judge a man, and yourself, without the trappings"],"terms":[]},{"i":874,"j":878,"loc":"Letter LXXXI 1–10","title":"Ingratitude and offsetting benefit with injury","summary":"To Lucilius, who has met an ingrate, Seneca says it is better to get no return than to give no benefits; one sows again after a bad crop. He refers to his work On Benefits and takes a new question: does a later injury cancel an earlier benefit? A strict judge balances the two, crediting the benefit's priority and the spirit of each act; a kinder judge forgets the injury. Seneca values a benefit above an injury and notes that the good man cheats himself in the benefit's favour. He explains that making a return means a voluntary giving, unlike paying a debt.","ideas":["Benefits must keep being given despite some ingratitude","Benefits and injuries depend on the spirit behind them","When the same man helps and harms, the benefit can outweigh the wrong","Only the wise man knows the true value of a benefit"],"terms":[{"t":"beneficium (benefit)","d":"A favour freely conferred, valued by the goodwill behind it rather than its bulk."},{"t":"referre gratiam (to make a return)","d":"Seneca's term for voluntarily repaying a favour, distinguished from paying back a debt."}]},{"i":879,"j":886,"loc":"Letter LXXXI 10–32","title":"Gratitude as its own reward","summary":"Seneca defends the Stoic paradox that only the wise man knows how to return a favour or confer one, noting that Metrodorus says the same. The good man weighs time, place and cause, leans toward the benefit, and allows for interest on what he owes. Gratitude benefits the grateful person more than its recipient, since the reward of virtue is virtue itself, while the ingrate is unhappy at once; Attalus says evil drinks most of its own poison. The wise man dwells on benefits and forgets wrongs. Seneca ends by noting that all peoples agree gratitude is honourable, though greed makes men forget benefits.","ideas":["The wage of a good deed is to have done it","Ingratitude torments the ingrate himself","Gratitude may require exile, blood or a stained reputation","Things are desired because praised, not praised because desirable"],"terms":[{"t":"paradoxa (Stoic paradoxes)","d":"Surprising Stoic claims, such as that only the wise man is a true friend or can return a favour."}]},{"i":887,"j":907,"loc":"Letter LXXXII","title":"Death is indifferent, but must be faced","summary":"Seneca tells Lucilius that his soul is on safe ground, warns that leisure without study is a living tomb, and urges him to wall himself in with philosophy, since retirement cannot keep out fear. He mocks Zeno's syllogism that death is glorious and therefore no evil, and its counter-syllogism, arguing instead that death, like poverty or exile, is indifferent and becomes glorious only when virtue handles it, as with Cato against the base Brutus. Death still carries a semblance of evil because of our love of self and fear of the unknown, so the soul must be hardened by practice. He ends by contrasting Leonidas' blunt words at Thermopylae with petty logic, which cannot slay so great a monster as the fear of death.","ideas":["Philosophy is an impregnable wall against Fortune","Indifferent things take honour or baseness from the virtue or vice that handles them","Death is indifferent yet naturally feared, so it calls for long training","Dialectical subtleties cannot rouse courage; great monsters need great weapons"],"terms":[{"t":"adiaphora (indifferents)","d":"Things neither good nor evil in themselves, such as sickness, pain, poverty, exile and death."},{"t":"otium (leisure)","d":"Retirement, which Seneca says is a tomb for the living unless it is joined with study."}]},{"i":908,"j":920,"loc":"Letter LXXXIII","title":"A day reviewed, and Zeno on drunkenness","summary":"Seneca begins by saying we should live as though in plain sight, since God sees into our thoughts, and he reports his own day: rest, reading, a short run with the slave Pharius, a barely cold bath, plain bread and a brief nap amid the noise of the races. He then attacks Zeno's syllogism that the good man will not get drunk because no one entrusts secrets to a drunkard, parodying it and rejecting Posidonius' defence that Zeno meant the habitual drunkard. Examples of Tillius Cimber, Lucius Piso and Cossus show heavy drinkers trusted with secrets. Seneca concludes that drunkenness should instead be condemned openly as voluntary madness that exposes every vice, citing Alexander's killing of Clitus and Antony's cruelty.","ideas":["Daily self-review is a useful habit; God witnesses our minds","Zeno's syllogism against drunkenness is easily parodied and its premiss is false","Drunkenness does not create vice but brings it into view","Excessive pleasures become punishments; argue from facts, not words"],"terms":[{"t":"ebrietas (drunkenness)","d":"Called by Seneca a condition of insanity purposely assumed."}]},{"i":921,"j":930,"loc":"Letter LXXXIV","title":"Reading like bees: gathering and digesting ideas","summary":"Seneca says his journeys help his health and his studies, and that reading and writing should alternate. Following the example of bees, which gather from many flowers and transform what they gather, he urges Lucilius to sift his reading and blend it into something of his own. Like food that must be digested to nourish, borrowed thoughts must pass into the reasoning power, so that the imitator resembles his model as a child resembles a father, and many voices make one chorus. He closes with reason's call to abandon riches, pleasures and office, and to take the peaceful road of wisdom rather than the crowded doorways of the great.","ideas":["Reading must be digested into one's own reasoning, not merely memorized","The bee and the chorus as images of unity made from many sources","Imitate as a child resembles a father, not as a picture copies its original","Reason bids us abandon riches, pleasure and office for wisdom"],"terms":[]},{"i":931,"j":939,"loc":"Letter LXXXV, 1–16","title":"Against moderating the passions","summary":"Lucilius has asked for the syllogisms on whether virtue suffices for happiness, and Seneca, while disclaiming pleasure in such proofs, sets out the Stoic chain from prudence to happiness. Certain Peripatetics reply that the wise man is only rarely and moderately perturbed or saddened. Seneca rejects this: a wise man merely better than the worst is no wise man, health is not moderate illness, and passions of any size do not obey reason, grow with their external causes, and are easier to keep out than to control once admitted. He also rejects the claim that the wise man is calm in disposition yet sometimes struck by fear, since fear admitted becomes a vice and would make him go reluctantly to die for his country.","ideas":["The wise man must be wholly free of passions, not moderately so","A crowd of moderate passions is worse than one strong one","Passions arise from causes outside our control, so their extent is not controllable","It is easier to keep a passion out than to master it once in"],"terms":[{"t":"apatheia (freedom from passion)","d":"The Stoic ideal Seneca defends: the complete absence of passion in the wise man."}]},{"i":940,"j":947,"loc":"Letter LXXXV, 17–41","title":"Happiness admits no degrees; the pilot argument","summary":"Seneca distinguishes two theses, that only the honourable is good and that virtue suffices for happiness, noting Xenocrates, Speusippus and Epicurus. He argues the happy life is complete and admits no increase, since it holds the Supreme Good and is measured by fullness, not length. Against the objection that the brave man must fear real evils, he replies that bravery is knowledge of what is and is not evil, so the brave man feels pain but not fear. On the argument that pain and poverty do not make a man worse, he answers the pilot analogy: storms hinder the voyage, not the pilot's art, and the wise man's work goes on in any fortune, like Phidias working in any material or a tamer subduing wild beasts.","ideas":["The happy life is a unit and cannot be more or less happy","Bravery is knowledge distinguishing evil from what only seems evil","Fortune hinders the pilot's success, not his art; the wise man always acts","The wise man makes something noteworthy of whatever fortune he finds"],"terms":[{"t":"summum bonum (Supreme Good)","d":"The highest good, which admits no degree above itself and so cannot be increased."},{"t":"fortitudo (bravery)","d":"Defined as the knowledge that distinguishes what is evil from what is not."}]},{"i":948,"j":964,"loc":"Letter LXXXVI","title":"Scipio's villa and transplanting olive trees","summary":"Writing from the country-house of Scipio Africanus at Liternum, Seneca praises his moderation in going into voluntary exile rather than threaten Rome's liberty. He describes the house's walls, towers and small dark bath, and contrasts Scipio's rough bathing with the marble, glass, silver spigots and sunlit windows of modern baths, noting that the old Romans bathed fully only once a week yet smelled of camp, farm and heroism. He then reports what the estate's owner Aegialus taught him about transplanting old olive trees and vines, correcting Vergil's farming advice on sowing beans and millet. He breaks off so as not to train Lucilius into a rival.","ideas":["Scipio's self-exile shows moderation and duty toward the laws","Luxurious modern baths contrasted with the austere bath of Scipio","Old trees can be transplanted: a lesson for old men planting for successors","Vergil wrote to please readers, not to instruct farmers"],"terms":[]},{"i":965,"j":973,"loc":"Letter LXXXVII, 1–11","title":"Travelling light with a farmer's cart","summary":"Seneca describes two days on the road with his friend Maximus, with a cartload of slaves, a mattress on the ground, dried figs and writing tablets. He admits he still blushes when a grander party passes his farmer's cart, proof that his principle is not yet firmly settled in him. He argues that men are judged by their trappings when they should be reckoned by what remains after debts, including debts to Fortune. He holds up Cato the Censor riding a single laden donkey against modern dandies with outriders, then turns to the Stoic syllogisms on virtue.","ideas":["How much we own is superfluous and easily given up","Seneca's embarrassment shows his own progress is incomplete","A man with great possessions on loan from Fortune is poor","Cato's donkey outshines a dandy's whole retinue"],"terms":[{"t":"impedimenta (hindrances)","d":"Baggage; Seneca says whoever first named superfluous things so foresaw what they would become."}]},{"i":974,"j":988,"loc":"Letter LXXXVII, 12–41","title":"Syllogisms proving riches are not goods","summary":"Seneca gives Stoic syllogisms that chance events and wealth are not goods and answers Peripatetic objections. What is good makes a man good, as the art and not the instrument makes the musician; what can fall to a pander or a eunuch like Chelidon is no good; good cannot spring from evil, and the gain of sacrilege is blended with the crime, unlike gold beside a serpent in a jar. He prefers Posidonius' view that riches are an antecedent cause of evil, puffing up the mind, and his distinction between advantages, which may be mixed, and goods, which must be pure. After Antipater's treatment of the poverty argument, he concludes that it is better to subdue desires by conduct than to circumvent them by logic.","ideas":["The good is what makes its possessor good; wealth does not","Goods cannot come from evil, as figs do not grow on olive trees","Riches are an antecedent, not an efficient, cause of evil","Advantages may contain harm; a good must be unmixed"],"terms":[{"t":"causa antecedens (antecedent cause)","d":"A cause that goads men toward evil without itself producing the harm, as Posidonius says riches do."},{"t":"commoda (advantages)","d":"Things with more usefulness than annoyance, distinct from goods, which have no harmful element."}]},{"i":989,"j":1001,"loc":"Letter LXXXVIII, 1–17","title":"Liberal studies do not teach virtue","summary":"Asked about liberal studies, Seneca says the only truly liberal study is wisdom, which makes a man free; the rest are apprenticeship. The grammarian's questions about Homer's philosophy, Ulysses' wanderings or Penelope's chastity do nothing to remove fear or desire, and Seneca asks to be taught instead how to love country and family and what purity is. He makes the same demand of the musician, the geometer, who measures fields but not how much is enough, and the astronomer, whose knowledge of the stars cannot change destiny. He ends by saying he is ready for whatever tomorrow brings.","ideas":["Only the study of wisdom truly deserves the name liberal","Homer is made a Stoic, Epicurean, Peripatetic and Academic, so is none","Each art should teach the corresponding thing in the soul","Knowledge of the stars cannot help us escape what is fated"],"terms":[{"t":"studia liberalia (liberal studies)","d":"Studies worthy of a free-born man; Seneca says only philosophy truly frees."}]},{"i":1002,"j":1014,"loc":"Letter LXXXVIII, 18–46","title":"The arts prepare the soul, philosophy perfects it","summary":"Seneca excludes painting, sculpture and wrestling from the liberal arts and grants that the liberal arts prepare the soul for virtue without bestowing it. He reports Posidonius' fourfold division of the arts and denies that mathematics is part of philosophy, since it builds on borrowed first principles, while only knowledge of good and evil perfects the soul. Bravery, loyalty, temperance and kindliness are not taught by such studies, and wisdom needs room for questions about time and the soul. He condemns excessive learning, from Didymus' four thousand books to Apion's theory about Homer, and the philosophers' own subtleties, from Protagoras and Parmenides to the sceptical schools.","ideas":["Liberal arts set the soul going toward virtue but do not bring it there","Mathematics aids philosophy but is not part of it","Wanting to know more than enough is a kind of intemperance","Some philosophers give useless knowledge; others abolish knowledge altogether"],"terms":[{"t":"enkyklios paideia (cycle of studies)","d":"The Greek round of boys' education, which Romans call liberal."}]},{"i":1015,"j":1029,"loc":"Letter LXXXIX","title":"The divisions of philosophy","summary":"Seneca agrees to divide philosophy into parts but not scraps, first distinguishing wisdom, the perfect good of the mind, from philosophy, the love and pursuit of it, while noting that some Stoics hold the two inseparable. He reports the threefold division into moral, natural and rational philosophy, and the variants of Peripatetics, Epicureans, Cyrenaics and Aristo of Chios. Moral philosophy is split into appraising values, impulse and action; natural into the bodily and non-bodily; rational into rhetoric and dialectic. He stops short of further subdivision and ends with denunciations of greed for land, luxurious villas and gluttony, reminding Lucilius that all study must be applied to conduct.","ideas":["Wisdom is the goal; philosophy is the journey toward it","Philosophy is moral, natural and rational","Moral philosophy weighs values, regulates impulse and orders action","Everything read or heard must be applied to conduct"],"terms":[{"t":"sophia (wisdom)","d":"The perfect good of the human mind, which philosophy seeks."},{"t":"rhetoric and dialectic","d":"The two parts of rational philosophy: continuous speech and speech divided between questioner and answerer."}]},{"i":1030,"j":1045,"loc":"Letter XC, 1–19","title":"Philosophy and the golden age, against Posidonius","summary":"Seneca says life is the gods' gift but living well is philosophy's, which each must win for himself. He accepts Posidonius' view that in the golden age wise men ruled as a service and later framed laws, like Solon and Lycurgus. But he denies that philosophy invented houses, tools or metal-working: these came from ingenuity and luxury, and Diogenes breaking his cup is wiser than Daedalus inventing the saw. Nature supplied what is necessary with little effort, and luxury, turning away from her, made the soul a slave to the body.","ideas":["Living well is the gift of philosophy, which no one receives ready-made","In the golden age the wise ruled; later they framed laws","The crafts were discovered by ingenuity, not wisdom","Nature provides the necessary; only luxuries require labour"],"terms":[{"t":"aurea aetas (golden age)","d":"The early period in which, Posidonius says, the wise governed."}]},{"i":1046,"j":1056,"loc":"Letter XC, 20–33","title":"What wisdom really discovered","summary":"Seneca says Posidonius' eloquence led him astray in attributing weaving, farming, milling and baking to the wise. Reason devised these things, but not right reason; they were found by the kind of men who still practise them, and recent devices like window-glass and shorthand were invented by slaves. Wisdom instead trains minds, works for peace, shows what is evil and what only seems so, and initiates us into the temple of the universe, the gods, the soul and truth. Inventions credited to Anacharsis or Democritus were made by them as men, not as wise men.","ideas":["The crafts come from reason, but not from right reason","Wisdom is the artisan of life, not of tools","Wisdom's rites open the vast temple of the universe","A wise man may invent things, but not by virtue of his wisdom"],"terms":[{"t":"recta ratio (right reason)","d":"Reason in its perfected form, which Seneca says did not invent the useful arts."}]},{"i":1057,"j":1066,"loc":"Letter XC, 34–46","title":"The innocent first men were not wise","summary":"Seneca says the wise man discovered truth, nature and the law of life, teaching men to follow the gods and count only the honourable as good. He describes the age when nature's gifts were common, before avarice broke the human fellowship and brought poverty, and men slept peacefully under open skies watching the stars. Yet those men were not wise: they were innocent through ignorance, possessing qualities akin to the virtues but not virtue itself. Virtue comes only to a soul trained, taught and perfected by practice.","ideas":["Avarice destroyed common ownership and created poverty","The first men lived in peace under the open sky","Innocence from ignorance differs from refusing to sin","Nature gives the stuff of virtue; virtue is an art to be learned"],"terms":[{"t":"avaritia (avarice)","d":"Greed, which Seneca says broke the primitive community and introduced poverty."}]},{"i":1067,"j":1079,"loc":"Letter XCI","title":"The burning of Lyons and expecting misfortune","summary":"Seneca's friend Liberalis is grieved by a fire that destroyed the colony of Lyons in a single night. Seneca says the unexpected weighs most heavily, so we must think beforehand not of what usually happens but of the worst that can happen, since Fortune overturns cities and empires in an instant and even mountains crumble. He suggests Lyons may rise again better, as Timagenes said of Rome's fires. All are equal before the law of dissolution; death and pain are feared only through common opinion, which is no more worth fearing than gossip, and death frees many from suffering.","ideas":["Rehearse in advance the greatest evil that can happen","All human works, and even nature's, are doomed to perish","We are unequal at birth but equal in death","The fear of death rests on rumour, like fear of gossip"],"terms":[{"t":"forethought of evils","d":"The practice of considering in advance every misfortune that might occur."}]},{"i":1080,"j":1087,"loc":"Letter XCII, 1–16","title":"Happiness rests on perfect reason alone","summary":"Seneca argues that the soul's ruling part is reason, derived from divine reason, so the happy life depends solely on perfect reason and its peace of mind. He rejects Antipater and others who let externals or pleasure add to the Supreme Good, calling the mixture of virtue and pleasure a monstrous hybrid like Vergil's Scylla. Health, rest and neat clothing may be chosen because they accord with nature, but the good lies in the choice, not the thing chosen. Against those who say the virtuous man is happy but not completely happy without health, he argues that if virtue can save a man from wretchedness it can make him supremely happy.","ideas":["The happy life is the attainment of perfect reason","Adding pleasure to virtue is like adding a lamp to sunlight","The good is in the quality of the selection, not the thing selected","Virtue that lifts a man from misery can complete his happiness"],"terms":[{"t":"ataraxia (undisturbedness)","d":"The quiet of mind the Greeks name, which Seneca says the soul attains by contemplating nature."},{"t":"secundum naturam (according to nature)","d":"Said of health and similar things, which may be chosen though they are not goods."}]},{"i":1088,"j":1102,"loc":"Letter XCII, 17–35","title":"Virtue undimmed, and the soul's divine kinship","summary":"Seneca compares virtue under misfortune to the sun behind clouds, its power unchanged. He rejects the claim that a wise man in bodily suffering is neither happy nor wretched, arguing that the lukewarm-water analogy fails and that virtue must either hold its ground or fall entirely; Epicurus himself called a day of agony his happiest. Against those who reserve virtue and happiness for the gods, he says reason is shared by gods and men and the soul, a part of God, strives to return to its origin. Such a soul despises wealth, oversees the body without serving it, and cares nothing for its corpse, as Maecenas' verse on burial suggests.","ideas":["Obstacles hide virtue's light but do not lessen it","No middle state between happiness and wretchedness for the wise","Reason is common to gods and men; the soul seeks to return heavenward","The freed soul cares no more for its body than a newborn for the afterbirth"],"terms":[{"t":"ratio (reason)","d":"Perfected in the gods and capable of perfection in men."}]},{"i":1103,"j":1111,"loc":"Letter XCIII","title":"Life measured by quality, not length","summary":"Responding to Lucilius' lament for the philosopher Metronax, Seneca says we treat men fairly but rail unfairly at Fate. A life is long if it is full, and fullness comes when the soul has gained control of itself: an idle man of eighty has merely existed, while a young man who fulfilled every duty lived a complete life. Seneca would accept more years but lacks nothing essential if denied them, since wisdom is the most important goal. The wise man departs bravely whether he goes to the gods or to nowhere, and a short life, like a slim book, may outweigh the bulky Annals of Tanusius.","ideas":["We should strive to live rightly rather than long","A life is complete when it is full, whatever its length","Wisdom is life's most important goal, not its farthest","The wise man is unafraid whether death leads to the gods or nowhere"],"terms":[]},{"i":1112,"j":1121,"loc":"Letter XCIV, 1–17","title":"Aristo's case against moral precepts","summary":"Seneca opens the question of the 'preceptorial' department of philosophy, which gives advice for particular roles such as husband, father or master. Some value it alone; Aristo the Stoic thinks it trivial, holding that knowledge of the Supreme Good lets a man frame his own precepts, while Cleanthes calls it useful but feeble unless derived from general principles. Seneca then sets out Aristo's arguments at length: precepts are useless while false opinions blind the mind, clear precepts need no adviser and doubtful ones need proofs, the cases are endlessly many, and giving rules to the mentally diseased is like advising a madman how to walk. The objector concludes that the whole department should be abolished.","ideas":["Advice for particular roles versus knowledge of life as a whole","Aristo: remove false opinions and precepts become superfluous","Precepts are either superfluous to the knower or insufficient to the ignorant","Folly as a madness that must be cured at its cause"],"terms":[{"t":"praecepta (precepts)","d":"Particular rules of conduct for given persons and situations, as opposed to the general doctrines of philosophy."},{"t":"decreta / dogmata (doctrines)","d":"The general principles of philosophy, such as the definition of the Supreme Good, from which conduct is derived."}]},{"i":1122,"j":1137,"loc":"Letter XCIV, 18–52","title":"Seneca's defence of advice and precepts","summary":"Seneca answers Aristo point by point. Nature gives eyesight but does not teach duty in every case; precepts refresh the memory, fix attention on what is known but neglected, and help the progressing even if they cannot cure hardened vice. Pithy maxims and verses strike the emotions directly and fan the seeds of virtue in the soul; association with good men works like a subtle sting. Against Posidonius he defends Plato's preambles to laws, and argues that virtue has a contemplative part taught by training and a practical part taught by admonition. Those not yet wise need to be guided like boys tracing letters, so this department is not superfluous.","ideas":["Precepts remind and focus attention rather than teach","Maxims stir natural seeds of virtue","Virtue divides into contemplation of truth and conduct","The learner must be guided before he can guide himself"],"terms":[{"t":"admonitio (admonition)","d":"Advice that rouses and directs attention to duties already known in principle."},{"t":"proficiens (one making progress)","d":"The person not yet wise who still needs to be told 'avoid this' or 'do that'."}]},{"i":1138,"j":1144,"loc":"Letter XCIV, 52–74","title":"A guardian voice against the crowd","summary":"Seneca argues that we need a preceptor to counter the harmful wishes, curses and opinions of parents, slaves and neighbours, since our faults come from without rather than from nature. Nature buried gold and silver underfoot and raised our gaze to the heavens; we dug up the causes of our own ruin. The guardian voice should tell us not to envy the powerful, and Seneca shows that Alexander, Pompey, Caesar and Marius were driven by greed and ambition, hounded while they seemed to hound others. Vices need an audience, so retirement and a wise adviser cure them; the powerful turn to philosophy only in adversity.","ideas":["Vices are acquired from society, not inborn","Nature hid metals and displayed the heavens","Conquerors as men conquered by their own desires","Display feeds vice; prosperity removes good sense"],"terms":[{"t":"natura (Nature)","d":"The standard to which wisdom returns us, which produced men healthy and free from vice."}]},{"i":1145,"j":1159,"loc":"Letter XCV, 1–29","title":"Precepts alone insufficient; luxury breeds diseases","summary":"Lucilius asks whether the paraenetic department alone can make a man wise, and Seneca promises a long letter as playful revenge. He answers arguments that precepts suffice: they work only on a receptive will, and wisdom differs from other arts because it concerns life as a whole and a voluntary error is worst in it. Philosophy, being theoretic as well as practical, needs doctrines, as roots to branches. To the claim that older, simpler wisdom made better men, he replies with an extended comparison to medicine, describing how luxurious and mixed foods have multiplied diseases, even making women gouty and bald.","ideas":["Wisdom is an art of living that concerns the whole of life","Doctrines relate to precepts as elements to members","Simple vices needed simple cures; complex luxury needs complex remedies","Catalogue of diseases caused by gluttony"],"terms":[{"t":"paraenetic (preceptorial) philosophy","d":"The part of philosophy that gives practical advice and exhortation."},{"t":"dogmata (doctrines)","d":"Theoretical tenets of an art or school, which Seneca says philosophy cannot do without."}]},{"i":1160,"j":1172,"loc":"Letter XCV, 29–53","title":"Doctrines needed against public madness","summary":"Seneca applies the medical comparison to philosophy: men are now mad nationally, praising wars and arena slaughter that would be punished in individuals, so philosophy must use stronger remedies. Conduct must be regulated by doctrines, with men bound to virtue as soldiers to their oath; precepts fail while greed, fear or lust hold us back, and right acts done without principle are matters of luck. The same act, such as sitting by a sick friend, is honourable or base by its purpose, as the story of Tiberius's mullet illustrates. He then gives a short account of worshipping the gods by right belief and imitation, and of human fellowship: we are parts of one great body, like stones in an arch.","ideas":["War and the arena as collective madness","Precepts need the support of a firm doctrine about the good","Worship the gods by being good, not by rituals","Humanity as one body; the verse 'I am a man'"],"terms":[{"t":"summum bonum (Supreme Good)","d":"The goal set before our eyes to which all acts and words should refer, like a sailor's star."}]},{"i":1173,"j":1182,"loc":"Letter XCV, 54–73","title":"Valuing things, knowing virtue, and examples","summary":"Seneca continues: we must judge the true value of poverty, riches, renown and exile, and must know what virtue is, whether one or many, since right conduct depends on right will and right will on a mind with a fixed standard of truth. Precepts without doctrines wither like leaves without a branch, and even those who reject doctrines state a doctrine in doing so. He grants Posidonius that persuasion, consolation, aetiology and 'ethology', the description of the marks of virtues, are useful. Vergil's lines on a thoroughbred colt become a portrait of Cato, and the letter ends with exemplary Romans, above all Tubero's earthenware at a public feast.","ideas":["A fixed standard of judgment brings peace of mind","Rejecting doctrines is itself a doctrine","Doctrines are hidden roots, precepts the visible branches","Cato and Tubero as living models of virtue"],"terms":[{"t":"aetiology","d":"Investigation of causes, which Posidonius adds to the tools of moral teaching."},{"t":"ethology (characterization)","d":"Description of the signs and marks of each virtue and vice, giving virtue's embodiment rather than its rule."}]},{"i":1183,"j":1195,"loc":"Letters XCVI–XCVII","title":"Accepting hardship; vice belongs to every age","summary":"In Letter XCVI Seneca tells the complaining Lucilius that the only evil is his chafing: illness, losses and fear are the tax of life, and Seneca has trained himself to agree with God's decisions; life is a battle, not a café. Letter XCVII argues that vices belong to mankind, not to the present age, citing the bribery and sexual corruption of the jury in the Clodius trial, which Cicero describes, all done in Cato's presence. Sin brings pleasure rather than shame, yet everyone hides it, showing an inborn sense of the good. Seneca accepts Epicurus's saying that the guilty can never be sure of remaining hidden, while rejecting his denial of natural justice: the punishment of crime lies in the crime and in the fear it brings.","ideas":["Willing obedience to God; troubles as the tax of life","The Clodius trial: acquittal bought with adulteries","Concealment of sin proves a natural knowledge of right","Conscience punishes crime with unending fear"],"terms":[{"t":"conscientia (conscience)","d":"Inner awareness of guilt that convicts wrongdoers to themselves and keeps them afraid."}]},{"i":1196,"j":1204,"loc":"Letter XCVIII","title":"Goods of fortune are not our own","summary":"Seneca argues that joy from external things is fragile; Fortune gives only the raw material of good and ill, and the soul makes them one or the other. One should foresee reverses and hold children, wife and property as things one may lose, without suffering in anticipation. Metrodorus is quoted that all mortal goods are mortal; only wisdom and virtue last, and what we have had can never be taken away. Examples such as Mucius, Regulus, Socrates, Fabricius and Tubero show that suffering and wealth can be mastered. The letter ends with an ailing old friend who endures pain and awaits death without fleeing either.","ideas":["The soul, not Fortune, makes things good or bad","Grief at loss and fear of loss are equal","To have may be taken away, to have had never","Philosophy proved in a friend's endurance of pain"],"terms":[{"t":"fortuna (Fortune)","d":"Chance, which supplies external things but cannot bestow true goods or evils."}]},{"i":1205,"j":1218,"loc":"Letter XCIX","title":"Rebuke to Marullus on his son's death","summary":"Seneca encloses a letter to Marullus, who was grieving excessively for a small son, choosing rebuke over gentle consolation. He argues that the past is secure, that death is the common lot, that the dead have only gone ahead, and that life is neither good nor evil but the place where both exist, so the child lost only a hazard. He does not demand hardness: some tears are forced by nature and some sweet tears come with memory, but grief should not be performed for onlookers. He attacks Metrodorus's idea of seeking a pleasure akin to sadness, urges remembering the dead without mourning, and notes that the dead cannot suffer.","ideas":["Gratitude for past possession instead of mourning loss","Even a long life is nothing beside eternity","Natural tears versus tears staged for an audience","Remember the dead, but cease to mourn"],"terms":[{"t":"Metrodorus's 'pleasure akin to sadness'","d":"The Epicurean view, which Seneca rejects, that one should pursue a pleasure mixed with grief while mourning."}]},{"i":1219,"j":1228,"loc":"Letter C","title":"Defending the style of Fabianus","summary":"Lucilius has criticized the style of Fabianus Papirius's work on the duties of a citizen, and Seneca defends him. Fabianus's words flow rather than tumble, chosen but not hunted for; he was building character rather than words and writing for the mind, not the ear. Seneca contrasts Cicero's even, gradual style with Asinius Pollio's abrupt one, and ranks Fabianus just below Cicero, Pollio and Livy. Fabianus lacks the orator's sting and epigrams but is calm, lucid and sincere, and from memory of his lectures Seneca recalls that his style inspired young men to emulate him without despair.","ideas":["A philosopher should not be timid about words","Fluent, assured style versus fashionable mannerism","Eloquence should follow the subject like a shadow","Teaching that inspires emulation without despair"],"terms":[]},{"i":1229,"j":1243,"loc":"Letter CI","title":"Senecio's sudden death and Maecenas's prayer","summary":"Seneca tells how Cornelius Senecio, a thrifty knight on the verge of wealth, died of quinsy overnight after a normal day, to show the folly of long plans when no one owns tomorrow. Death stands beside all our projects, so we should postpone nothing, balance life's account daily and treat each day as a separate life; one whose life is complete each day does not fear the future. He then condemns verses of Maecenas praying to keep life even if maimed and crucified, calling a lingering death no life at all. The letter concludes that what matters is not how long but how nobly one lives.","ideas":["Sudden death mocks far-reaching hopes","Count each day as a separate life","Maecenas's prayer as base clinging to life","Nobility of life matters more than length"],"terms":[]},{"i":1244,"j":1250,"loc":"Letter CII, 1–19","title":"Is posthumous renown a good?","summary":"Lucilius's letter interrupts Seneca's pleasant dream of the soul's immortality, asking him to answer the dialecticians' objection that renown cannot be a good because it consists of separate persons' opinions. Seneca distinguishes continuous, composite and distinct bodies, and states that no good consists of distinct things. He answers that renown can rest on one good man's judgment, since all good men judge alike; that praise is a judgment, not mere utterance; and that renown differs from glory, which depends on the many. The good of deserved praise belongs both to the praised and to those who praise, since praising the worthy is an act of justice.","ideas":["Three kinds of bodies: continuous, composite, made of distinct members","One good man's verdict equals that of all good men","Praise lies in judgment rather than speech","Renown (claritas) versus glory (gloria)"],"terms":[{"t":"claritas (renown)","d":"The favourable judgment of good men, which Seneca argues is a good."},{"t":"gloria (glory)","d":"Fame resting on the judgments of the many, distinguished from renown."}]},{"i":1251,"j":1258,"loc":"Letter CII, 20–30","title":"The soul's homeland and its birth into eternity","summary":"Dismissing such quibbles, Seneca turns to the greatness of the soul, whose homeland is the whole universe and which claims all time as its own, held for now in an earthly prison. As the womb prepares us for birth, so life prepares us for another birth; the body is luggage in an inn, and the day feared as the end is the birthday of eternity. He describes the soul shedding flesh like an afterbirth and seeing the full light of heaven. Such thoughts, he says, make one fearless and mindful of the gods as witnesses, and even one who thinks the soul perishes is helped by the memory of great men.","ideas":["Dialectical subtleties distract from philosophy's majesty","Life as a gestation for a second birth","Death as the birthday of eternity","Hope of immortality makes the soul fearless"],"terms":[]},{"i":1259,"j":1275,"loc":"Letters CIII–CIV, 1–19","title":"Danger from men; travel cannot heal","summary":"Letter CIII warns that men, not accidents, are our everyday danger, and urges harmlessness, sympathy and refuge in philosophy, practised without arrogance. In Letter CIV Seneca has gone to Nomentum to shake off a fever, partly for his wife Paulina's sake, arguing that a good man should sometimes live on for those who love him. Country air restores him, but he insists that place matters little: as Socrates said, a man travels with himself. False values of wealth, office and death follow us everywhere, and travel offers only novelty and geography, not cure; one needs medicine, not scenery, and study with masters of wisdom.","ideas":["Man is most dangerous to man","Living on for the sake of loved ones as kindness","You need another personality, not another place","Travel informs but does not make us better"],"terms":[]},{"i":1276,"j":1285,"loc":"Letter CIV, 21–34","title":"Socrates and Cato as patterns of endurance","summary":"Seneca advises changing companions: live with the Catos, Laelius and Tubero, or with Socrates, Zeno, Chrysippus and Posidonius. Nature gave man a lofty spirit that seeks honour rather than security, and toil and death are dreadful only to look upon; difficulty comes from lack of confidence. Socrates endured poverty, a difficult home, war, the Thirty and the democracy, and his features never changed even at the poison. Cato stood alone for the Republic between Caesar and Pompey, marched through African deserts, played ball after an electoral defeat and chose his own death. To be free one must reject pleasure and spurn wealth.","ideas":["Choose the great dead as companions","Fear arises from lack of confidence, not difficulty","Socrates unchanged by Fortune","Cato's endurance; wealth as the diploma of slavery"],"terms":[]},{"i":1286,"j":1298,"loc":"Letters CV–CVI","title":"Living safely; is the good a body?","summary":"Letter CV gives precepts for safety: men are driven to harm others by hope, envy, hatred, fear and contempt, which can be avoided by having nothing conspicuous, provoking no one, being easily reconciled, and talking little; above all, never doing wrong, since the guilty conscience never has ease. In Letter CVI Seneca answers whether the good is corporeal: the good is active, what acts is a body, and emotions, vices and virtues visibly change the body, so they too are bodies, for only body can touch body, as Lucretius says. He then dismisses such arguments as a game that makes men clever, not good, and laments excess in learning.","ideas":["Five sources of injury and how to avoid each","Silence as protection; secrets always spread","Stoic proof that virtues and emotions are corporeal","Learning for the lecture-room rather than for life"],"terms":[{"t":"corporeal good","d":"The Stoic thesis that the good, being active, must be a body."}]},{"i":1299,"j":1311,"loc":"Letter CVII","title":"Runaway slaves and obedience to fate","summary":"Lucilius is upset because his slaves ran away, and Seneca reproaches him: life is like a public bath or a journey, full of knocks, and nothing should surprise one who has foreseen it. Every misfortune has befallen many, and what is laid down for all is an equal law. Nature governs by alternating seasons and opposites, and we must follow God without grumbling, like a good soldier. Seneca renders Cleanthes's hymn to Zeus in Latin, ending with the thought that fate leads the willing and drags the unwilling.","ideas":["Anticipation removes the shock of misfortune","An equal law is what is laid down for all","Eternity consists of opposites","Cleanthes's prayer: the willing are led by fate"],"terms":[{"t":"fatum (fate)","d":"The ordained course of the universe to which the good man willingly yields."}]},{"i":1312,"j":1329,"loc":"Letter CVIII, 1–23","title":"Seneca's youthful zeal under Attalus and Sotion","summary":"Before sending his books on moral philosophy, Seneca advises Lucilius to regulate his eagerness to learn, recalling Attalus's teaching. A true pupil takes home one good thing each day, while many come to lectures only for pleasure or phrases; verses and maxims strike the mind harder than prose, as Cleanthes compared to a trumpet. Attalus's attacks on luxury led Seneca to give up oysters, mushrooms, perfume, wine and hot baths, and to use a hard pillow. Sotion explained the vegetarianism of Sextius and Pythagoras, including transmigration, and Seneca abstained from meat for a year until his father, wary of suspicion of foreign cults under Tiberius, made him stop.","ideas":["Learn what you can hold, not all you wish","Hearers who seek pleasure versus true learners","Poetic form gives moral ideas greater force","Seneca's abstinence and his year without meat"],"terms":[{"t":"metempsychosis (transmigration)","d":"Pythagoras's doctrine, as Sotion reports it, that souls pass from one body to another, including animals."}]},{"i":1330,"j":1353,"loc":"Letter CVIII, 23–38","title":"Reading as a philosopher, not a grammarian","summary":"Seneca blames teachers who teach debate rather than living and pupils who seek wit rather than soul, so that the study of wisdom has become the study of words. He contrasts how a scholar and a philosopher read Vergil's line that time flies: the philosopher draws the lesson that our best days come first and must be seized, while the scholar notes word usage. Likewise a philologist reading Cicero's On the State collects antiquarian facts and archaic words, such as the Ennius verses borrowed from Homer. Seneca urges that all reading serve the happy life, that words become deeds, and that a teacher who does not live as he preaches is useless, like a seasick pilot.","ideas":["The same text yields different matter to different readers","Vergil's 'time flies' read as a call to act","Philosophy must be lived, not marketed","Words become one's own by doing them"],"terms":[]},{"i":1354,"j":1362,"loc":"Letter CIX","title":"Whether one wise man can help another","summary":"Lucilius asks how a wise man, who already possesses every good, can be helped by another. Seneca answers that good men help each other by exercising one another's virtues, as wrestlers and musicians are kept sharp by equals, by sharing discoveries, and by mutual joy. He answers objections that the helped man really helps himself, or that perfect heat needs no added heat, by noting that helping is of many kinds and that perfect reason is prompted only by perfect reason. In practical affairs the wise also take counsel together. He closes by calling such questions mental gymnastics and asking philosophy to make him braver and more just instead.","ideas":["Bad men corrupt each other; by the same logic good men improve each other","Even the wise man is not all-knowing and benefits from a friend's counsel","Only perfect reason can prompt perfect reason","Seneca demands useful teaching over subtle puzzles"],"terms":[{"t":"indifferentia (indifferents)","d":"Things like money, influence and security, which some say help us but which Seneca does not count as real help to the wise."}]},{"i":1363,"j":1383,"loc":"Letters CX–CXII","title":"True riches, quibbles, and a hardened friend","summary":"Writing from Nomentum, Seneca says the worst curse is to be at enmity with oneself and that so-called evils often help us; our fears are empty because no one examines them, and he corrects Lucretius: we are not afraid in daylight but have turned everything to darkness. Nature put useful things within reach and buried harmful ones, which greed digs up. He quotes Attalus, who lost his craving for riches after seeing a lavish display and concluded that one must crave nothing to rival Jupiter. Letter CXI renders Greek sophismata as Cicero's cavillationes and says they make no one braver; the true philosopher is like a mountain that looks greater close up. Letter CXII, using vine-grafting as an image, warns that Lucilius' pampered friend only thinks he wants reason.","ideas":["Distinguish what is necessary, which is everywhere, from the superfluous, which must be hunted","Attalus: riches are displayed, not possessed, and are despised for their pettiness","Life is scarcely long enough to learn to despise life, which comes before controlling it","Men love and hate their vices at once; a quarrel with luxury is not a break with it"],"terms":[{"t":"cavillationes (sophismata)","d":"Cicero's Latin word for logical tricks or quibbles, which Seneca says bring no progress toward real living."}]},{"i":1384,"j":1397,"loc":"Letter CXIII","title":"Are the virtues living things?","summary":"Seneca sets out the Stoic argument that virtue, being the soul in a certain condition and possessing impulse, is a living thing, and then states his dissent. He argues that justice is a state of the soul, not a separate being; that many living things cannot share one soul or body; that virtues are equal while no two living things are; and that virtue lacks its own assent. He mocks the consequences: wise silence, dining, gaits and lines of Vergil would be living things. Noting that Cleanthes and Chrysippus disagreed about walking, he claims his own freedom, then turns to what matters: bravery as a fortress against Fortune, Alexander mastered by his emotions, and justice practised without reward or renown.","ideas":["A virtue is a power or state of one soul, not a separate creature","The absurd consequences of the doctrine show it is idle hair-splitting","Posidonius: fight Fortune with your own weapons, not hers","Self-command is the greatest command; be just even when it brings disgrace"],"terms":[{"t":"impetus (impulse)","d":"The movement toward action that the Stoic argument says only living things possess; Seneca replies that in justice it belongs to the soul."},{"t":"assensio (assent)","d":"The rational approval that confirms an impulse before action, which Seneca says virtue itself cannot give."}]},{"i":1398,"j":1412,"loc":"Letter CXIV","title":"Style as a mirror of character","summary":"Asked why corrupt styles of speech arise, Seneca cites the Greek proverb that a man's speech is like his life: a soul's condition shows in its gait and its eloquence, and a fashionable decadent style reveals a luxurious age. He takes Maecenas as his example, quoting his tortured phrases and linking them to his loose dress and effeminate habits. He traces how luxury moves from dress to furniture, houses, banquets and finally words, lists faults of archaism, bombast, broken or sing-song rhythm, and shows how Arruntius slavishly copied Sallust's mannerisms. He concludes that the soul is king: care for it, remember mortality, and measure needs by one small belly.","ideas":["Corrupt speech that pleases the public signals corrupt public morals","Maecenas' style matched his ungirt life","Imitating another's mannerisms multiplies faults, as Arruntius did with Sallust","The soul rules like a king or, when overcome by pleasure, a tyrant"],"terms":[{"t":"animus as rex (the soul as king)","d":"Seneca's image, with a Vergil quotation on bees, of the soul as the ruler whose health keeps all other functions in order."}]},{"i":1413,"j":1450,"loc":"Letters CXV–CXVI","title":"False glitter of wealth; against moderate passions","summary":"Seneca tells Lucilius to care for what he writes, not how; polished style betrays a petty mind. He imagines the radiant face of a good man's soul, worthy of worship, and contrasts it with the gilded veneer of wealth and rank, which we prize like children prizing pebbles. He blames parents, public opinion and poets for teaching us to revere gold, quoting tragic lines on money and the audience's hissing of Euripides' Bellerophon. Philosophy offers the true boon of freedom from regret. Letter CXVI argues against the Peripatetic view that moderate emotions are acceptable: every passion starts weak but grows, so it is easier to keep it out, as Panaetius advised a youth about love; our real problem is unwillingness, not inability.","ideas":["Style is the garb of thought","Gold-leaf prosperity hides cheap wood beneath","Greed is its own penalty; possession brings more agony than acquisition","Stoics reject the emotions, Peripatetics moderate them; Seneca sides with rejection"],"terms":[{"t":"adfectus (emotions, passions)","d":"Disturbances of mind that Seneca says cannot safely be held to a moderate amount once admitted."}]},{"i":1451,"j":1461,"loc":"Letter CXVII, sections 1–17","title":"Is being wise a good, like wisdom?","summary":"Lucilius asks about the Stoic claim that wisdom is a Good but being wise is not. Seneca explains the school view: the Good is corporeal because it acts, while being wise is incorporeal and accessory to wisdom. He then dissents, citing common consent, and argues that whatever affects something is corporeal, that being wise can belong only to the good man and so is neither evil nor indifferent, and that it is the use of wisdom, without which wisdom would be worthless. He reports the dialecticians' distinction between body and statements about body, as in 'Cato walks', and the analogy of a field and its possession, then shows that wisdom and its possessor share one nature.","ideas":["Stoics hold the Good is corporeal because it is active and helpful","General agreement of mankind counts as evidence, as for belief in gods","Being wise is the employment of wisdom and therefore desirable and good","A field changes owners by law, but wisdom never leaves its possessor"],"terms":[{"t":"corporale (corporeal)","d":"Bodily; the Stoics count the Good as corporeal because only bodies act or are acted upon."},{"t":"enuntiativum (statement, 'utterance')","d":"What is said about a body, like 'Cato walks', which the dialecticians treat as incorporeal."}]},{"i":1462,"j":1467,"loc":"Letter CXVII, sections 18–33","title":"Leave the quibbles, learn how to live","summary":"Seneca rebukes himself for arguing a plain matter and says such questions lie on the outskirts of wisdom, while study of gods and stars at least uplifts the mind. He asks instead to be taught how to bear sorrow and prosperity and how to leave life by his own choice when proper. He condemns praying for death, since death is already in our power. He dismisses the question whether future wisdom is a Good: what is yet to be is not yet helpful. With images of a man running for a midwife or fleeing a burning house, he insists that short life leaves no time for knots, and that philosophy should be a remedy, not entertainment.","ideas":["Praying for death is base; dying is a necessity, choosing the time a privilege","What is yet to come is not yet a Good, since it cannot help now","Time is too short to spend on useless questions","Philosophy should make us braver and Fortune's superior"],"terms":[]},{"i":1468,"j":1476,"loc":"Letter CXVIII","title":"Canvassing for nothing; defining the Good","summary":"Seneca declines Cicero's habit of filling letters with election gossip and prefers to examine how many vain things he himself is a candidate for. The noble course is to canvass for nothing and tell Fortune he asks no favours. Goals look great from below but prove mean when reached, a rung rather than a summit. He then reviews definitions of the Good, rejecting 'what attracts' and preferring what rouses impulse in accordance with nature and is fully worth seeking. He distinguishes the honourable, good in itself, from things made good by it, and argues that the natural becomes the Good through magnitude, as a keystone completes an arch.","ideas":["Freedom lies in seeking no office or favour from Fortune","The Good is connected with the true, not with what merely seems true","The honourable is good of itself; indifferent pursuits become good through it","Growth can change a thing's kind, not just its size"],"terms":[{"t":"honestum (the honourable)","d":"The perfect Good from which other things derive their goodness and which can never be anything but good."},{"t":"secundum naturam (according to nature)","d":"A mark of the Good, though Seneca holds that only what is completely according to nature is good."}]},{"i":1477,"j":1487,"loc":"Letter CXIX","title":"Nature as the best provider","summary":"Seneca playfully promises Lucilius a quick road to riches: borrow from yourself, as Cato said, since craving nothing and possessing something both bring freedom from worry. Nature must be given her due but asks only that hunger and thirst be satisfied, not how or from what vessel. He states his find: the wise man is the keenest seeker of nature's riches. Having enough gives a stopping-point the rich never reach; Alexander remained poor after conquering Darius. Riches grip their owners like a fever, and the Builder of the universe provided for well-being, not luxury, so necessity spares us squeamishness.","ideas":["Enough is never too little, and not-enough is never too much","Money breeds a greater craving for itself","Measure all things by the demands of nature, which are cheap","What we need from necessity we accept without fussiness"],"terms":[]},{"i":1488,"j":1500,"loc":"Letter CXX","title":"How we form the idea of virtue","summary":"Lucilius asks how we first know the good and the honourable. Seneca says nature gives only seeds of knowledge; the Stoics hold the concept arises by analogy, as from bodily health we inferred health of mind. Admiring deeds like Fabricius refusing Pyrrhus' gold and warning him of poison, or Horatius Cocles at the bridge, gave us a picture of virtue, while look-alike vices such as prodigality forced careful distinctions. The idea of perfect virtue came from a man consistent in every act who accepted his lot as a soldier's orders and treated the body as an inn. Seneca ends by condemning inconsistency, citing Horace's ever-changing man: only the wise man plays one role.","ideas":["The concept of the good is reached by analogy from observed deeds","Vices neighbour virtues and must be told apart","Consistency is the proof of true virtue; unsteadiness the mark of an evil mind","The soul that judges its present home narrow shows its loftier origin"],"terms":[{"t":"analogia (analogy)","d":"Inference from the familiar to the unseen, by which the Stoics say we reach the concept of the honourable."}]},{"i":1501,"j":1511,"loc":"Letter CXXI","title":"Animals' awareness of their own constitution","summary":"Anticipating Lucilius' objection that this has nothing to do with character, Seneca argues that knowing human nature is necessary for knowing what to do. He then takes up whether all animals feel their own constitution, pointing to their born skill in moving their limbs, the child learning to stand and the tortoise righting itself. He answers that a child need not grasp a definition of constitution to sense its own, just as we feel we have souls without knowing their nature. Each age is adapted to its present state, and every creature is first entrusted to itself. Chicks fearing hawks and cats, spiders' webs and bees' cells show inborn self-preservation rather than learning.","ideas":["Animals are born skilled in using their bodies","Self-awareness can be confused and dim yet real","Each creature is adapted to the constitution of its current age","Self-love and avoidance of harm are inborn, not taught"],"terms":[{"t":"constitutio (constitution)","d":"The ruling part of the soul in relation to the body, which every animal senses in itself."},{"t":"conciliatio (adaptation, self-attachment)","d":"Nature's commitment of each creature to itself, the basis of seeking what helps and avoiding harm."}]},{"i":1512,"j":1529,"loc":"Letter CXXII","title":"Those who turn night into day","summary":"As days shorten, Seneca praises rising with the dawn and attacks those who reverse light and darkness, Antipodes within the city who never see sunrise or sunset. Their banquets are like funerals and their bodies grow flabby like birds fattened in the dark. All vices rebel against nature: drinking on an empty stomach, men dressing as women, roses in winter, forests on roofs. He tells anecdotes of Acilius Buta, whom Tiberius said woke too late, the jokes about him at Montanus' readings, and Sextus Papinius' nightly round reported by Pedo Albinovanus. The root cause is a craving for notoriety and disgust at the normal; he urges Lucilius to follow nature, not row against the current.","ideas":["The proof of life lies in action; lengthen life by using the night","Luxury delights in the unusual and in opposing nature","The evil conscience hates daylight and seeks to be talked about","Virtue is simple; vice has endless forms"],"terms":[]},{"i":1530,"j":1538,"loc":"Letter CXXIII","title":"Resisting the voices of pleasure","summary":"Arriving late at his Alban villa with no meal ready, Seneca tests his own temper and finds that hunger will make even bad bread good. A test that comes on suddenly proves strength better than a prepared one, and many things prove superfluous only when missing; we live by convention rather than reason. He warns against talk that praises vice, which sticks like a catchy tune, and gives the hedonist's speech in full: eat, drink, spend, for life is short. Lucilius should shun such voices as Ulysses shunned the Sirens, resist what allures and meet what attacks, and reject false Stoics who make the wise man an expert lover and drinker. He ends with sound maxims to learn by heart.","ideas":["A good-humoured stomach is a step toward independence","Wrong views, once common, come to seem right","Lean back on pleasure's downhill slope, lean forward up virtue's climb","No man is good by chance; virtue must be learned"],"terms":[]},{"i":1539,"j":1556,"loc":"Letter CXXIV and Appendix","title":"The Good is grasped by reason, not senses","summary":"Seneca asks whether the Good is grasped by the senses or the understanding. Against those who make pleasure supreme, he argues that if the senses judged, no pleasure would be refused and no one blamed for slavery to appetite; the dullest faculty would judge the highest. The natural endowment at birth is only the beginning of a Good, so infants, animals and plants have no true Good, only a good by courtesy; man's Good is a free, upright mind ruled by perfect reason, as in God. Animals live in the present and their impulses are confused. He urges Lucilius to stop competing with beasts in strength and beauty. An appendix summarises the terms of Letters XCIV–XCV on doctrines and precepts.","ideas":["Pleasure-philosophers put the treetop where the root should be","Four natures: tree, animal, man, God; only the reasoning ones admit the Good","Animals perceive only the present, rarely recalling the past","Count as most unfortunate those the world calls fortunate"],"terms":[{"t":"ratio perfecta (perfect reason)","d":"The Good proper to man, a clear and flawless mind rivalling God's."},{"t":"praecepta and decreta (precepts and doctrines)","d":"The appendix's pair: practical advice for average duty and the principles behind absolute duty, both needed."}]}]});